WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:04.530
Good afternoon.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.571
Good afternoon.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.020
I'm Rafael Reif.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.080
I'm MIT's president.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.610
And I am delighted, and
it's a privilege for me,

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.800
to welcome you all to
this important event.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.419
Let me just do a
microphone check.

00:00:20.419 --> 00:00:21.710
Do you hear me way in the back?

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.920
Do you hear me all right?

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.720
Our subject today
is the report called

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.450
"Sexual Harassment of Women--

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.000
Climate, Culture,
and Consequences

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.000
in Academic Sciences,
Engineering, and Medicine."

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.220
As you would expect
from a Consensus

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.370
Report of the
National Academies,

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:46.910
it is a serious
piece of scholarship.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.190
And we are fortunate
that its lead authors--

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:52.280
President of Wellesley
College, Paula Johnson,

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.760
and Institute Professor
Sheila Widnall of MIT--

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.180
are with us today.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.960
I'd like to start by offering
a warm welcome to friends

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.120
and colleagues from
our academic neighbors,

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.420
including Wellesley
and Brandeis,

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.600
and a special welcome
to our guest speaker,

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.610
University Professor Anita Hill.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.050
I also want to recognize--

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.990
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.810
My only job here today
was to introduce her.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.050
I also want to recognize
Chancellor Cindy Barnhart,

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:51.470
Provost Marty Schmidt, Vice
President and General Counsel

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:55.280
Mark DiVincenzo, Vice President
for Human Resources Lorraine

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.590
Goffe, and then many
others here today

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:03.990
who have led the way, officially
and unofficially, over decades,

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.620
to make MIT a more equitable
and welcoming community

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.060
for everyone.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.490
Thank you.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.594
And by all means, keep going.

00:02:15.594 --> 00:02:19.110
Today, I'm deeply encouraged.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:22.170
Your attendance speaks
to the strong desire

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.050
to understand these
issues more fully

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:28.140
and to keep making progress.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.740
In a moment, Sheila and Paula
will speak about their findings

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.690
and recommendations.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.390
For now, I'll offer
just a few comments

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:43.190
on why these issues matter
so much right here at MIT.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:46.310
Over the last year,
the Me Too movement

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:51.650
forced the whole country to
grasp that sexual harassment is

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.780
pervasive.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:57.610
Like all of you, I was deeply
disturbed by the revelations

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.330
of misconduct elsewhere.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:05.910
And I must believe it
also happens at MIT.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:12.720
On this question, our community
is not an oasis of safety.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.690
In fact, the National
Academies' report

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:18.050
makes these same points
about academic institutions

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.940
in general.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.310
When it comes to
sexual harassment,

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:27.920
a community like ours presents
a particular set of risks--

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.150
a 24/7 environment that
brings together people

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:36.840
across a broad range of ages,
incomes, and backgrounds,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:40.810
many of whom have
power over others--

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:46.400
the power to make being at
MIT miserable, power enough

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.480
to make or break a career.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.020
The success of our community
is built on collaboration

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.020
and mutual respect.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.530
Sexual harassment is
an act of aggression

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.400
that belittles,
unnerves, and controls.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.160
It violates our
fundamental expectations

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:12.110
of respect and equality,
and it violates the humanity

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:15.850
of the person being harassed.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:18.560
At MIT, fulfilling
our mission depends

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.310
on people of exceptional
talent performing

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:24.800
at the height of
their capacities

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.970
and working together to solve
the most challenging problems.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:34.980
It's obvious that there can be
no room for sexual harassment

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:35.860
in that picture.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.180
Last fall, I wrote
to the community

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.380
to express those
values and ideas.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:48.060
And I also described our efforts
to fight sexual harassment

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.200
at MIT.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:54.560
Let me offer a few
updates since then.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.040
We have expanded rules
regarding consensual relations

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.850
among community members,
across lines of authority,

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:05.180
such as faculty and
graduate students.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.490
We are assessing the
disciplinary process

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:12.180
for handling sexual
misconduct cases.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:15.270
Marty, Cindy, Mark,
and Lorraine have

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.790
worked to develop
enhanced processes

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:21.810
for handling sexual harassment
complaints against staff

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:23.450
and faculty.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:26.450
And these recommendations
are being vetted internally,

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.905
and I hope that we can start
to implement them soon.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:35.400
Because a true solution
needs to include everyone,

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:39.860
we have also greatly
expanded training efforts.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:43.000
Online, we started
with students.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.400
And a week ago, Marty,
Cindy, and Lorraine

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:49.450
let us know that when we
expanded that online training

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:53.330
to include all paid
MIT faculty and staff,

00:05:53.330 --> 00:06:00.850
the participation rate
was an outstanding 99.7%.

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:03.610
To the 9,300 faculty
and staff who

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:08.810
took the time to complete
this program, I thank you.

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:12.560
This is a tremendous expression
of this community's commitment

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.280
and values.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.030
Finally, to make good
decisions, we need good data.

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:22.130
MindHandHeart is
already conducting

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.680
studies about the working
and learning environment

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.230
in various departments.

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:30.440
And next spring,
we will once again

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:33.650
conduct an in-depth survey
that will allow students

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:36.650
to convey how they
may have experienced

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:40.890
sexual misconduct at MIT.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:43.440
I'm grateful to
everyone who has worked

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.360
so hard on these many fronts.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.630
In the end, however,
the most important work

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:54.360
is up to all of us.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:57.780
We need to actively
build a culture that

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.660
treats sexual
harassment, coercion,

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:05.430
and assault as out of bounds--

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:12.230
unthinkable for anyone of
any age in any context.

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:16.610
Getting the culture right
matters to us here on campus.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:19.820
And it matters far
beyond campus too.

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:22.280
Because the students
we educate will go on

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.750
to launch, and lead, and
populate companies, labs,

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:31.410
agencies, institutions, and
governments around the globe.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:34.850
And they will carry with
them the professional culture

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:39.080
they experience at MIT.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:42.240
Finally, let me
state the obvious.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:47.510
Most harassers are men.

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:52.630
As a result, the
men in our community

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:55.660
have a particular
responsibility and must

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:58.000
play a particularly
important role

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:03.770
in leading and driving the
necessary change in culture.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:07.240
Every member of our
community is valuable.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:12.030
And harm to one is harm to all.

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:14.190
As long as sexual
harassment and assault

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:17.270
persists in our
community, we fail

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.870
to live up to our
shared potential

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:25.660
and to fulfill our aspiration
to make a better world.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:29.580
So I close with a challenge
that we each strive

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:34.140
to define what we can do
to invent a better MIT

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:38.010
community for those
who are here today

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:40.799
and for those who
follow us tomorrow.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:48.330
And now, it is my great
honor to introduce someone

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:53.340
whose integrity and courage,
27 years ago, brought

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:58.740
sexual harassment to the center
of our national conversation.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:02.790
In 1991, during the confirmation
hearings for US Supreme Court

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:05.820
nominee Clarence
Thomas, Anita Hill

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:09.870
testified that, as her
supervisor at the US Department

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:13.680
of Education and the Equal
Employment Opportunity

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:19.320
Commission, Clarence Thomas
had sexually harassed her.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:22.440
For those of us who were
adults at that time,

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:26.220
Professor Hill's courage
in coming forward,

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:30.390
and the terrible disrespect she
endured during her testimony

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:35.610
and long afterward,
are unforgettable.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:37.410
Until that moment,
sexual harassment

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.650
was a legal and academic term.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:42.260
In fact, early
work on the subject

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:47.160
was done here at MIT by
our very own Mary Rowe.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:51.400
But the concept was
not widely understood.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:55.330
Today, we know that sexual
harassment is pervasive,

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:59.290
that it's wrong, and that
the responsibility to stop it

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:03.140
belongs to all of us.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:06.080
For all that, we owe a
great debt of gratitude

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:10.010
to University
Professor Anita Hill.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:13.350
A graduate of Yale Law School,
she's the author of two books,

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:18.550
including her autobiography,
Speaking Truth to Power.

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:20.800
Today, she serves as
the University Professor

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:23.830
of Social Policy, Law,
and Women's Gender,

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:27.440
and Sexuality
Studies at Brandeis.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:30.830
And last fall, she was selected
by some leading figures

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:34.310
in Hollywood to chair
their new Commission

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:37.680
on Sexual Harassment
and Advancing Equality,

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:41.210
which aims to tackle the broad
culture of abuse and power

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:44.940
disparity in the
entertainment industry.

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:49.300
Professor Hill spent last
year as an MIT MLK scholar.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:52.500
She continues as an
MIT research affiliate.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.470
And we're very
proud to count her

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:58.300
as a member of our community.

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:01.470
Please join me in offering
a warm welcome to University

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.268
Professor Anita Hill.

00:11:03.268 --> 00:11:06.754
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:19.204
Good evening.

00:11:19.204 --> 00:11:21.196
Good afternoon.

00:11:21.196 --> 00:11:24.184
Thank you.

00:11:24.184 --> 00:11:30.550
Thank you, President Reif, for
that gracious introduction.

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:32.800
And thank all of
you for being here

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:38.470
to hear the report on this
incredibly impressive study

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:42.610
that I have already
begun relying on,

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:47.860
not only in my own work,
but in my teaching as well.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:50.460
I'm really pleased
to be here at MIT.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:52.300
I say I'm pleased to be back.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:54.130
I was here last year.

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:57.810
But I'm especially pleased to
be here for today's program

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:02.170
on the National Academies
Consensus Study Report.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:04.930
My first experience
with this study

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:09.790
came late in the year of 2016.

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:12.370
And that was at the
initial convening

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:14.860
of this really impressive
group of people

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:20.110
who have come together to
contribute to the work that

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:23.620
has resulted in the
publication that you're just

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:25.450
about to get a glimpse of.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:29.600
I will tell you that
you will need more time,

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:37.010
and actually a lot more
time, because this report is,

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:39.250
I would say, the most
comprehensive report

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:41.740
on sexual harassment
in the research

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:47.640
and the recommendations that has
been published to this point.

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:53.970
And we are, of course, thrilled
that the co-chairs of the study

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:59.090
group, President Paula Johnson
of Wellesley University

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:06.230
and your own Sheila Widnall,
Institute Professor at MIT,

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:10.750
are here to present to us today.

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:14.800
This work actually set me--
the beginning of this work--

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:17.290
set me in good stead
for my year here

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:22.930
at MIT in the research
laboratory of electronics

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:25.210
as an MLK fellow.

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:28.390
And I have the pleasure of
partnering with Professor

00:13:28.390 --> 00:13:34.930
Muriel Médard and her whole
laboratory, actually--

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:37.370
I felt like I was partnering
with the entire lab,

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:42.430
including our administrative
assistant, Molly Kruko,

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:45.670
Sampson Wilcox,
who created the art

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:51.160
for our series for this poster,
as well as a series of talks

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:57.080
that we presented to this
community about Title IX,

00:13:57.080 --> 00:14:00.670
but more broadly about
gender and racial inclusion

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:07.250
in science, technology,
engineering, and medicine.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:10.840
So we hosted this series, and
we called it the Gender Race

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:12.180
Imperative.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.380
Because I realized that
gender inclusion was never

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:21.890
going to be complete unless we
include it in our thinking--

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:24.530
other identity factors as well.

00:14:24.530 --> 00:14:27.020
And our talks were
focused really

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:28.980
on greater inclusion in STEM.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:31.160
And really not just
the greater inclusion,

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:33.230
but how do we get there?

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:35.220
Not only talking
about the problem,

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:38.340
but how do we get to a solution.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:39.440
Now, I will say--

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:41.600
and this could sound
immodest, but it's

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:43.460
not really immodest
because I didn't

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:45.980
give any of the lectures--

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:52.100
but the entire series
was quite excellent,

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:57.530
covering topics including law,
of course, the history of women

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:01.160
at MIT, which was a
lecture given by Bob Gray,

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:08.060
mentoring, given by Institute
Professor Leslie Kolodziejski,

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:13.550
and marginalization, and a
lecture given by Dr. Ellen

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:16.640
Hammond of Harvard.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:19.460
There were many others as well.

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:23.030
And the tie-in to
today's program, I think,

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:26.060
will become much more obvious
as we go on for today.

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:29.750
But I do want you to know
that if you miss the lectures,

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:32.490
they are on our website.

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:40.690
And you can find them at
rle.mit.edu/anita-hill.

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:43.770
So no topic is more
important or timely

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:46.190
than the topic of sexual
harassment and gender

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:49.730
harassment throughout society.

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:53.390
We are fortunate that the
National Academy has taken up

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:57.390
the challenge of exploring
this extensive problem.

00:15:57.390 --> 00:16:00.500
The contributions
that this volume makes

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:03.500
goes beyond science,
engineering, and medicine--

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:06.710
and beyond the
academic experience.

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:09.830
This consensus report
covers the issue,

00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:12.750
whether it is in the workplace
as a workplace issue,

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:14.690
whether it's in
school, whether we're

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:19.070
talking about students or
people well into their career.

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:21.050
You will find
information that is

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:28.990
relevant to sexual harassment,
whether men are the victims,

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:34.870
women are the victims, or
whether non-binary individuals

00:16:34.870 --> 00:16:36.160
are the victims.

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:40.810
So the range of this
work is really important.

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:44.110
But it's specifically
back to this setting.

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:48.770
In 2018 across the country
in universities and colleges

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:51.310
throughout, decisions
about the enforcement

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:56.620
of Title IX's protections are
being made, even as we meet.

00:16:56.620 --> 00:17:00.220
It reminds me of
a statement that

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:07.569
was made by Senator Birch
Bayh when Title IX was first

00:17:07.569 --> 00:17:11.500
enacted in 1972.

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:17.069
He admonished us that
Title IX was not a panacea.

00:17:17.069 --> 00:17:21.490
But he said, "It is," and I will
quote, "an important first step

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:24.940
in the efforts to provide for
the women of America something

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:28.030
that is rightfully
theirs, an equal chance

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:30.250
to attend the schools
of their choice,

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:33.340
to develop the skills
they want, and to apply

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:35.560
those skills with the
knowledge that they will have

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:39.340
a fair chance to secure
jobs of their choice

00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:43.370
with equal pay for equal work."

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:46.490
Now, what that also
means is that they

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:50.240
will be able to do
their jobs and attend

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:55.330
those schools in an environment
free from harassment.

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:57.820
But Title IX really
is just the start.

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:02.270
And the report brings that
out-- it's just the beginning.

00:18:02.270 --> 00:18:08.170
This volume-- if Title
IX is the first step--

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:13.090
this volume is a
critical next step.

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:18.330
It provides guidance for
protecting against harassment.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:21.120
It makes sure that women
in science, engineering,

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:25.020
and medicine are treated
fairly, from the school

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:33.250
of their choice, to equal pay
for equal work and in between.

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:41.440
By eliminating harassment,
women, men, and all individuals

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:46.900
have a better chance
of achieving equality.

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:50.390
Viewing harassment
as a systemic problem

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:56.300
is one of the greatest
contributions of this volume.

00:18:56.300 --> 00:18:58.340
And there are many,
many more that I

00:18:58.340 --> 00:19:02.690
won't get into, because that's
what our presenters are here

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:04.690
to do.

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:09.000
But I will say to all of
you who are here today,

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:12.150
whatever your
particular focus is,

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:15.390
whatever your particular
interest in the subject is--

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:17.590
whether it's
workplace harassment,

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:22.500
harassment in the academy,
harassment on the streets--

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:28.470
this volume has
recommendations, it has science,

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:33.420
and it also gives us
comfort in knowing

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:36.040
that change is possible.

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:39.810
And so with that, I will
turn the podium over

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:46.898
to Professor Widnall for a
presentation on the volume.

00:19:46.898 --> 00:19:49.832
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:01.620
Let me check that the
microphone is working.

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:03.280
Can you hear me way
in the back row?

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:05.040
OK, good.

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:07.320
OK, before I start, I
would like to introduce

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:09.960
two people that are here.

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:13.980
Our study director, Frazier
Benya, can you raise your hand?

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:16.330
She flew all the way
up from Washington, DC.

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:21.180
She was the person who kept
us in track on our report.

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:22.840
We couldn't have
done it without her.

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:24.900
And then Kate Clancy, who
is from the University

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:27.150
of Illinois, and she was
a member of our committee.

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:29.170
And she is spending
this year at MIT.

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:32.050
So we're delighted to see you.

00:20:32.050 --> 00:20:36.760
So this is a discussion
of the report

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:39.790
from the National Academies
of Science, Engineering,

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:40.910
and Medicine.

00:20:40.910 --> 00:20:45.100
And I want to emphasize
this word, Consensus Report.

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:48.380
National Academy reports
have to be based on data.

00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:49.810
You can't just give opinions.

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:52.450
You really have to have
data, and it has to be firm,

00:20:52.450 --> 00:20:55.720
and you have to be able to
rely on it and stand on it.

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:58.790
So that's sort of the
requirements for a report.

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:02.230
So let me try to push this.

00:21:02.230 --> 00:21:04.490
I'm trying to change
the slide, guy.

00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:08.800
Now, wait a minute.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:09.950
I had it upside down.

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:16.160
I think one of the things
I want to start out with

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:18.680
is how did we get into this?

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:22.760
There is a standing committee
at the National Academies

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:24.950
on women in science,
engineering, and medicine.

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:26.300
And that's a standing committee.

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:29.900
They've been in place
for a long time.

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:31.700
And then every once
in a while, they

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:34.940
put a task together
and ask a subcommittee

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:39.110
to take up that task for a
sort of finite period of time.

00:21:39.110 --> 00:21:43.910
And so we were tasked by the
National Academies Committee

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:45.330
to do this study.

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:46.730
And we have--

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:49.000
Paula Johnson is the co-chair.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:50.240
I'm the co-chair.

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:52.970
And there's a list
of our members.

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:56.150
They were social scientists,
they were scientists,

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:59.070
they were engineers, they
were people from industry.

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:02.210
So it was a very,
very broad committee.

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:05.120
This is the statement
of our task.

00:22:05.120 --> 00:22:07.070
And just to make
things easy for you,

00:22:07.070 --> 00:22:11.270
I've just highlighted the
first three words of each task,

00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:13.210
and you can read it if
you read the report.

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:15.890
But we're supposed to
review the research.

00:22:15.890 --> 00:22:19.100
We're supposed to examine
existing information,

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:23.880
and we're supposed to identify
and analyze possible policy

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:25.440
strategies and practices.

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:28.730
So that was basically
our task, dealing

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:32.450
with the influence of sexual
harassment in academia

00:22:32.450 --> 00:22:34.710
on the career
advancement of women

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:37.860
in scientific, technical,
and medical workforces.

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:39.710
So that was our task.

00:22:39.710 --> 00:22:40.820
These were our sponsors.

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:44.510
We had considerable
help from our sponsors.

00:22:44.510 --> 00:22:48.170
And we met with our sponsors
at the end of our task.

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:51.020
And I would say their sponsors
were extremely pleased

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:55.320
and want to continue to
move forward in this area.

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:57.900
So I'm going to talk
about the findings.

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:02.590
And then my co-chair Paula will
talk about our recommendations.

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:06.540
First of all, I think it's very
important to get a definition.

00:23:06.540 --> 00:23:09.780
I mean, we've heard, in
our opening statements,

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:13.116
several statements
about sexual harassment.

00:23:13.116 --> 00:23:14.490
So I think it's
really important.

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:16.500
And I think one of
the important things

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:21.070
that our committee did is give
a very broad definition of this,

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:24.390
which really encompasses
a variety of behaviors.

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:26.370
And so it's really
important to focus on those,

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:30.650
because they really influence
the climate in a university.

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:32.430
I'm focusing on universities.

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:34.090
But it's not just
the universities,

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:35.000
it's the workforce.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.870
It's the entire population.

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:41.460
So we're interested
in gender harassment,

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:45.690
unwanted sexual attention,
and sexual coercion.

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:47.730
Now, obviously,
gender harassment

00:23:47.730 --> 00:23:50.430
isn't the most
pervasive of those.

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:52.800
And yet, in some
fundamental sense,

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:54.990
it is almost the most important.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:58.720
Because it completely
controls the environment

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:02.940
that's seen by women in science,
engineering, and medicine.

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:06.400
So that's really what
we're focusing on.

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:10.230
And so sexual harassing
behavior is considered illegal

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:12.930
when it creates a
hostile environment

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:17.620
or when it is quid pro
quo sexual harassment.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:19.750
This is one of the
most important figures.

00:24:19.750 --> 00:24:22.720
And I'm just going to leave
this up for a little while.

00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:24.980
When we hear the word
sexual harassment,

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:28.090
most people focus on the
part of the iceberg that's

00:24:28.090 --> 00:24:29.460
out of the water.

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:33.340
And these are clearly
extremely important things

00:24:33.340 --> 00:24:35.410
having to do with
physical assault,

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:38.740
having to do with quid
pro quo harassment.

00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:41.980
But the part that is
less clear to the public

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:44.720
is the part that's underwater.

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:46.820
And I don't know if
you can all read this

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:51.440
from where you're sitting,
but obscene gestures,

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:54.410
offensive sexual
teasing, insults--

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:56.390
women don't belong
in science, women

00:24:56.390 --> 00:24:58.520
don't belong in engineering--

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:02.420
sabotaging women's
equipment, calling people

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:04.220
by vulgar names--

00:25:04.220 --> 00:25:08.840
all of those things, in some
sense, are below the waterline.

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:10.850
And yet, in some
fundamental sense,

00:25:10.850 --> 00:25:14.270
these are the most
important definitions

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:17.120
of sexual harassment,
and these are the things

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:21.690
that universities really
need to ensure don't happen.

00:25:21.690 --> 00:25:23.450
And so that's
really, I think, one

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:27.410
of our very, very important
outcomes of our report

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:30.760
is to really emphasize
these things.

00:25:30.760 --> 00:25:34.630
So this is some of
our findings, that

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:37.570
talking about ambient
harassment and gender

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:41.980
harassment, that sexual
harassing behavior can either

00:25:41.980 --> 00:25:44.950
be direct, targeted
at an individual,

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:46.210
or it can be ambient.

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:53.430
It can create an unsafe,
uncivil, unwelcome environment.

00:25:53.430 --> 00:25:57.600
And when it occurs
severely or frequently,

00:25:57.600 --> 00:26:01.260
it can result in the same level
of negative professional and

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:05.790
psychological outcomes,
as can isolated instances

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:10.550
of serious sexual
harassment and coercion.

00:26:10.550 --> 00:26:13.800
We believe that sexual
harassment is common,

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:17.240
unfortunately, that it is
common in academic science,

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:20.420
engineering, and medicine,
and that the data--

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:25.470
and we are basing our
report on available data--

00:26:25.470 --> 00:26:30.140
the data shows that 50% of women
faculty and staff in academia

00:26:30.140 --> 00:26:32.030
experience sexual harassment.

00:26:32.030 --> 00:26:34.850
And here, I'm talking about
this very broad definition

00:26:34.850 --> 00:26:39.260
of sexual harassment--
an uncivil environment.

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:44.360
And we had extensive
surveys of students.

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:49.840
And this student survey showed
that between 20% and 50%

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:53.830
of students in science,
engineering, and medicine

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:58.830
experience sexual harassment
from faculty or staff.

00:26:58.830 --> 00:27:00.889
And this is some data.

00:27:00.889 --> 00:27:02.430
I don't know if you
can all see this.

00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:08.010
But some of those bars are
40% high, they are 30% high,

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:10.110
they're 20% high.

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:12.870
And they deal with these
various definitions

00:27:12.870 --> 00:27:17.550
of sexual harassment-- gender
harassment hostility, gender

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:22.770
harassment crude behavior, and
then unwanted sexual attention

00:27:22.770 --> 00:27:26.715
and sexual coercion, which is
obviously the most serious.

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:34.810
And we also looked at these what
I would call intersectionality,

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:39.070
that women of color
experience more harassment--

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:43.350
sexual, racial, or ethnic,
or some combination--

00:27:43.350 --> 00:27:45.740
than white women do.

00:27:45.740 --> 00:27:49.160
And women of color often
experience sexual harassment

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:51.950
that includes racial harassment.

00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:54.530
And sexual and gender
minority people

00:27:54.530 --> 00:27:56.900
experience more
sexual harassment

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:58.760
than heterosexual women do.

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:06.580
Another finding is that
this kind of behavior

00:28:06.580 --> 00:28:10.520
can impact not only
the individual person,

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:13.660
but it can have an
effect on bystanders,

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:18.160
and it can have an effect on
the entire research enterprise.

00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:21.280
And it can undermine women's
professional and educational

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:26.330
attainment, and their
mental and physical health.

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:28.060
It has adverse
effects that affect

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:30.490
not only the targets
of the harassment,

00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:33.880
but also the bystanders, the
coworkers, the working groups,

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:36.640
and the entire organization.

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:39.400
And the cumulative effect
of sexual harassment

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:43.090
is significant damage
to research integrity

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:47.900
and a costly loss of talent in
academic sciences, engineering,

00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:51.030
and medicine.

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:54.270
Two of the characteristics
that are most associated

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:56.460
with higher rates
of sexual harassment

00:28:56.460 --> 00:29:01.050
are, unfortunately,
male-dominated gender ratios

00:29:01.050 --> 00:29:04.170
and leadership, and an
organizational climate

00:29:04.170 --> 00:29:08.630
that communicates tolerance
of sexual harassment.

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:12.490
So we felt that organizational
climate is by far the greatest

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:16.000
predictor of the occurrence
of sexual harassment,

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:18.550
and ameliorating it
can prevent people

00:29:18.550 --> 00:29:21.530
from sexually harassing others.

00:29:21.530 --> 00:29:23.170
So what we are
really focusing on

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:28.570
is the creation of a civil,
welcoming, safe environment

00:29:28.570 --> 00:29:33.070
for everybody, but particularly
focused on women students,

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:35.860
women faculty.

00:29:35.860 --> 00:29:38.500
And I believe that that
is, in some sense, one

00:29:38.500 --> 00:29:44.270
of the most important
findings that we have made.

00:29:44.270 --> 00:29:46.480
To talk a little bit
about the legal system--

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:48.680
and Anita talked a
little bit about this--

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:52.880
it is not an adequate mechanism
for reducing or preventing

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:55.100
sexual harassment.

00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:59.000
We often refer to Title IX as
a platform, not a solution.

00:30:02.090 --> 00:30:05.030
Judicial interpretation
of Title IX

00:30:05.030 --> 00:30:08.000
has somehow incentivized
organizations

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:11.600
to create policies,
procedures, and training

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:16.130
on sexual harassment that
focuses on symbolic compliance

00:30:16.130 --> 00:30:21.440
with current law, which will
help them to avoid liability.

00:30:21.440 --> 00:30:24.680
But that's checking the
boxes, and that doesn't really

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:28.670
influence, and it doesn't really
indicate a real commitment

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:32.610
to improve the environment.

00:30:32.610 --> 00:30:36.750
So we are focused on
Title IX, Title VII.

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:40.440
And we believe that they
reflect an inaccurate assumption

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:42.600
that the targets of
sexual harassment

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:45.420
will always promptly
report the harassment,

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:48.390
without worrying
about retaliation.

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:51.750
And what we found was the
least common response for women

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:56.220
is to formally report
the harassing experience.

00:30:56.220 --> 00:30:59.280
And for many, this is due
to an accurate perception

00:30:59.280 --> 00:31:02.700
that they may
experience retaliation

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:07.920
or other negative outcomes
associated with their personal

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:09.130
and professional life.

00:31:09.130 --> 00:31:13.200
So in some sense, the
price is higher for them

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.210
than it is for the perpetrator.

00:31:17.910 --> 00:31:20.460
We also made
recommendations with respect

00:31:20.460 --> 00:31:23.310
to the federal government
and professional societies.

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:28.440
Many professional societies
have taken up this issue,

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:34.770
and they have put in place
rules for attending meetings,

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:40.050
and policies and procedures, and
value statements that encourage

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:43.560
a welcome and civil environment
at professional society

00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:44.170
meetings.

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:46.770
And there have been quite a
few professional societies

00:31:46.770 --> 00:31:48.400
that have done that.

00:31:48.400 --> 00:31:51.720
And we believe that they
do have the potential

00:31:51.720 --> 00:31:55.990
to be powerful
drivers of change.

00:31:55.990 --> 00:31:59.590
And so this is really one of
our most important observations,

00:31:59.590 --> 00:32:02.950
is that, really, it's a system
wide change to the culture

00:32:02.950 --> 00:32:07.870
and climate in higher education
that is required to prevent

00:32:07.870 --> 00:32:10.630
and effectively
address all three

00:32:10.630 --> 00:32:13.770
forms of sexual harassment.

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:17.450
So we found that changing the
organizational climate means

00:32:17.450 --> 00:32:20.660
creating a climate where
sexual harassment-- again,

00:32:20.660 --> 00:32:27.080
this broad definition
of sexual harassment--

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:29.090
where sexual harassment
is not perceived

00:32:29.090 --> 00:32:31.370
to be tolerated or permitted.

00:32:31.370 --> 00:32:33.440
And you know, I can take
you back to the iceberg

00:32:33.440 --> 00:32:36.840
and remind you what all
those little micro things are

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:42.950
that are microinsults that many
people feel are not important.

00:32:42.950 --> 00:32:45.220
They are important.

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:47.260
So the characteristics
of an organization

00:32:47.260 --> 00:32:50.560
with a permissive climate
towards this kind of behavior,

00:32:50.560 --> 00:32:53.890
this kind of sexual harassment,
Include the following--

00:32:53.890 --> 00:32:56.240
if there's a perceived
risk to victims

00:32:56.240 --> 00:32:58.680
for reporting
harassment, if there

00:32:58.680 --> 00:33:01.810
is a lack of sanctions
against offenders,

00:33:01.810 --> 00:33:05.080
and if there's a perception
that one's complaints will not

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:06.280
be taken seriously.

00:33:06.280 --> 00:33:13.720
These are important pieces of
the organizational climate.

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:17.410
So at this point, I'm going to
stop talking about our findings

00:33:17.410 --> 00:33:21.340
and turn the podium over
to my co-chair Paula

00:33:21.340 --> 00:33:23.230
Johnson, President
of Wellesley, who's

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:24.940
going to talk about
our recommendations.

00:33:24.940 --> 00:33:27.345
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:39.310
Sheila, thank you so much.

00:33:39.310 --> 00:33:44.150
And it is truly a pleasure
to be here with you today.

00:33:44.150 --> 00:33:46.630
And I said to Anita, you
know, we started this

00:33:46.630 --> 00:33:50.020
before the Me Too
movement started.

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:54.430
And here we are today
with the news still

00:33:54.430 --> 00:34:00.560
booming with these issues,
which is truly unfortunate,

00:34:00.560 --> 00:34:04.810
but I think points out the
importance of this work.

00:34:04.810 --> 00:34:07.540
I want to just recognize
Evelyn Hammond, who

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:11.050
is a member of the
Committee of the National

00:34:11.050 --> 00:34:13.389
Academies of Science,
Engineering, and Medicine,

00:34:13.389 --> 00:34:17.530
who actually helped to charge
us with doing this report.

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:20.227
So Evelyn, thank you to
you and the committee.

00:34:20.227 --> 00:34:22.667
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:29.300
So I just want to say
a bit about myself.

00:34:29.300 --> 00:34:33.590
Before becoming the president
of Wellesley College, I spent--

00:34:33.590 --> 00:34:40.070
in July of 2016, I spent 31
years in academic medicine.

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:42.550
I was a professor of medicine
at Harvard Medical School

00:34:42.550 --> 00:34:46.730
and a professor of Epidemiology
at the Harvard TH Chan

00:34:46.730 --> 00:34:49.730
School of Public Health.

00:34:49.730 --> 00:34:54.590
And I did work on
research focused

00:34:54.590 --> 00:34:57.050
on the discovery of sex
differences in health

00:34:57.050 --> 00:34:59.180
and disease, and
translating that research

00:34:59.180 --> 00:35:00.625
into clinical care.

00:35:00.625 --> 00:35:02.000
And I do want to
say it has truly

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:05.240
been an honor to serve
on this committee,

00:35:05.240 --> 00:35:09.380
with Sheila as my co-chair, and
with Kate Clancy, who's here,

00:35:09.380 --> 00:35:13.580
and Frazier Benya, and the
rest of my committee members.

00:35:13.580 --> 00:35:18.710
So our report, in terms of its
most important conclusions,

00:35:18.710 --> 00:35:24.630
the real focus has been on how
to prevent sexual harassment.

00:35:24.630 --> 00:35:28.490
And I just want to
reiterate the point

00:35:28.490 --> 00:35:32.120
that Sheila made, that
a system-wide change

00:35:32.120 --> 00:35:35.600
in the culture and climate
in higher education

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:40.370
is required to prevent and
effectively address all three

00:35:40.370 --> 00:35:44.660
forms of sexual harassment,
sexual coercion,

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:49.410
unwanted sexual attention,
and gender harassment.

00:35:49.410 --> 00:35:51.870
And I want to point
out that in spite

00:35:51.870 --> 00:35:56.560
of significant attention to
this issue in recent years,

00:35:56.560 --> 00:35:59.700
there's absolutely no
evidence to suggest

00:35:59.700 --> 00:36:03.870
that current policies,
procedures, and approaches have

00:36:03.870 --> 00:36:08.410
resulted in a significant
reduction in sexual harassment.

00:36:08.410 --> 00:36:12.090
So it is really time
to really consider

00:36:12.090 --> 00:36:15.480
an approach that addresses
the system's cultures

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:22.770
and climates that enable sexual
harassment to perpetuate.

00:36:22.770 --> 00:36:25.800
So the top four things
that institutions

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:29.460
can do to prevent
harassment include one,

00:36:29.460 --> 00:36:33.150
to integrate values of
diversity, inclusion,

00:36:33.150 --> 00:36:37.750
and respect into
policies and procedures.

00:36:37.750 --> 00:36:40.990
Number two is to change
the power dynamics

00:36:40.990 --> 00:36:44.130
in advisor-trainee
relationships.

00:36:44.130 --> 00:36:48.600
Number three, to support
targets of sexual harassment

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:52.890
by providing alternative ways
to access support services,

00:36:52.890 --> 00:36:55.980
record information
about an incident,

00:36:55.980 --> 00:37:00.750
and report an incident
without fear of retaliation.

00:37:00.750 --> 00:37:05.580
And four, to improve
transparency and accountability

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:09.360
to demonstrate that
institutions are investigating

00:37:09.360 --> 00:37:11.754
and holding people accountable.

00:37:11.754 --> 00:37:13.920
So what I'm going to do is
go through some top-level

00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:16.800
recommendations,
and then drill down

00:37:16.800 --> 00:37:19.620
on the recommendations
for institutions,

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:25.140
and then drill down on the
recommendations for policy

00:37:25.140 --> 00:37:27.560
and federal agencies.

00:37:27.560 --> 00:37:32.690
So one, to pay attention to
and enact policies on gender

00:37:32.690 --> 00:37:34.290
harassment.

00:37:34.290 --> 00:37:37.970
It's important that leaders
and academic institutions,

00:37:37.970 --> 00:37:43.130
and research and training sites,
pay increased attention to

00:37:43.130 --> 00:37:46.520
and enact policies that
cover gender harassment

00:37:46.520 --> 00:37:48.230
as a means of
addressing the most

00:37:48.230 --> 00:37:52.550
common form of sexual
harassment and preventing

00:37:52.550 --> 00:37:56.180
other types of sexually
harassing behavior.

00:37:56.180 --> 00:38:01.130
It is clear that in environments
where gender harassment

00:38:01.130 --> 00:38:04.310
is allowed to grow,
allowed to flourish,

00:38:04.310 --> 00:38:07.130
that the other forms
of sexual harassment--

00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:11.330
unwanted sexual attention,
as well as sexual coercion--

00:38:11.330 --> 00:38:14.510
are more likely to occur.

00:38:14.510 --> 00:38:17.260
We have to move beyond
legal compliance

00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:19.990
to address culture and climate.

00:38:19.990 --> 00:38:23.680
Academic institutions,
research, and training sites,

00:38:23.680 --> 00:38:27.460
and federal agencies should
move beyond interventions

00:38:27.460 --> 00:38:31.780
or policies that represent
basic legal compliance

00:38:31.780 --> 00:38:36.070
and that rely solely on formal
reports made by targets.

00:38:38.780 --> 00:38:41.360
Three, professional
societies should

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:43.800
accelerate their efforts.

00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:45.450
This is an important
area, because we

00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:47.810
have seen movement in
professional societies,

00:38:47.810 --> 00:38:50.180
and we believe that
they can do more.

00:38:50.180 --> 00:38:53.360
Professional societies should
accelerate their efforts

00:38:53.360 --> 00:38:55.940
to be viewed as
organizations that

00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:59.810
are helping to create
culture changes that reduce

00:38:59.810 --> 00:39:04.760
or prevent the occurrence
of sexual harassment.

00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:07.700
And last, consider
sexual harassment

00:39:07.700 --> 00:39:12.470
equally important as
research misconduct.

00:39:12.470 --> 00:39:16.070
Academic institutions
and federal agencies

00:39:16.070 --> 00:39:19.880
should consider sexual
harassment equally important

00:39:19.880 --> 00:39:23.870
as research misconduct
in terms of its effect

00:39:23.870 --> 00:39:25.685
on the integrity of research.

00:39:28.640 --> 00:39:33.040
So when we think about
academic institutions,

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:36.590
it's important to-- as we think
about this culture change--

00:39:36.590 --> 00:39:40.450
it's important to create
a diverse, inclusive,

00:39:40.450 --> 00:39:42.880
and respectful environment.

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:44.140
Now that's easy to say.

00:39:44.140 --> 00:39:46.600
But what does that
actually mean?

00:39:46.600 --> 00:39:49.390
Academic institutions
and their leaders

00:39:49.390 --> 00:39:53.650
should take explicit steps
to achieve greater gender

00:39:53.650 --> 00:39:58.690
and racial equity in hiring and
in promotions, and as a result,

00:39:58.690 --> 00:40:03.010
improve the representation
of women and a diversity

00:40:03.010 --> 00:40:04.870
at every level.

00:40:04.870 --> 00:40:07.390
Academic institutions
and their leaders

00:40:07.390 --> 00:40:11.560
should take steps to
foster greater cooperation

00:40:11.560 --> 00:40:16.580
and respectful work behavior and
professionalism at the staff,

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:19.810
faculty, student,
and trainee levels,

00:40:19.810 --> 00:40:22.270
and should evaluate
faculty and staff

00:40:22.270 --> 00:40:26.970
on these criteria in
hiring and in promotion.

00:40:26.970 --> 00:40:30.900
Academic institutions should
combine anti-harassment efforts

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:33.720
with civility
promotion programs.

00:40:33.720 --> 00:40:35.550
And when we say
civility promotion,

00:40:35.550 --> 00:40:40.470
we are really talking
about an environment that

00:40:40.470 --> 00:40:43.950
fosters a culture of respect.

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:46.830
And as we think about
training, training

00:40:46.830 --> 00:40:49.430
should be tailored to
specific population.

00:40:49.430 --> 00:40:51.990
One size does not fit all.

00:40:51.990 --> 00:40:56.670
This is an area that really
requires more evaluation.

00:40:56.670 --> 00:41:00.660
But from the data, it's
clear that training

00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:03.750
does need to be tailored
to specific groups--

00:41:03.750 --> 00:41:07.240
students and trainees,
staff, faculty,

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:09.180
and those in leadership.

00:41:09.180 --> 00:41:11.730
And from the data
that we do have,

00:41:11.730 --> 00:41:14.100
it's clear that
training should not

00:41:14.100 --> 00:41:19.690
be focused on changing beliefs,
but on changing behavior.

00:41:19.690 --> 00:41:21.570
And there are some
qualities of training

00:41:21.570 --> 00:41:26.740
that also seem to be associated
with improved outcomes.

00:41:26.740 --> 00:41:30.210
Those are one, that
it's in person.

00:41:30.210 --> 00:41:33.940
Two, that it's a
reasonable length.

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:37.860
Three, that, again, targeted
to specific audiences.

00:41:37.860 --> 00:41:42.330
And four, that leadership
is present and involved

00:41:42.330 --> 00:41:43.050
in the training.

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:48.500
It makes a very clear
and strong statement.

00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:53.390
Second, it's important to
defuse the hierarchical power

00:41:53.390 --> 00:41:57.290
structure in the dependent
relationship between trainees

00:41:57.290 --> 00:42:03.390
and faculty, in order to reduce
the risk of sexual harassment.

00:42:03.390 --> 00:42:06.620
Now, some of the mechanisms
that academic institutions can

00:42:06.620 --> 00:42:11.210
consider include, for
example, mentoring networks,

00:42:11.210 --> 00:42:15.770
or committee-based advising,
and really consider

00:42:15.770 --> 00:42:19.160
departmental funding, as
opposed to funding only

00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:23.840
from one principal investigator,
which only enhances

00:42:23.840 --> 00:42:27.360
that dependent relationship.

00:42:27.360 --> 00:42:31.700
Next, we also recommend
providing support

00:42:31.700 --> 00:42:34.820
for the targets of
sexual harassment.

00:42:34.820 --> 00:42:37.010
Academic institutions
should convey

00:42:37.010 --> 00:42:39.740
that reporting
sexual harassment is

00:42:39.740 --> 00:42:42.567
an honorable and
courageous action.

00:42:42.567 --> 00:42:43.900
And I'm going to say that again.

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:47.750
It's an honorable and
courageous action.

00:42:47.750 --> 00:42:51.890
Regardless of whether
or not a target

00:42:51.890 --> 00:42:55.400
files a formal report,
academic institutions

00:42:55.400 --> 00:42:58.370
should provide ways
to access support

00:42:58.370 --> 00:43:01.940
services, such as
social services, health

00:43:01.940 --> 00:43:07.940
care, legal services, and career
and professional services.

00:43:07.940 --> 00:43:11.150
They should also
provide less formal ways

00:43:11.150 --> 00:43:15.050
of recording information
about the experience

00:43:15.050 --> 00:43:18.080
and reporting the
experience, particularly

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:24.230
if the target is not comfortable
with filing a formal report.

00:43:24.230 --> 00:43:28.250
And it's important that academic
institutions develop approaches

00:43:28.250 --> 00:43:31.850
to prevent the target from
experiencing or fearing

00:43:31.850 --> 00:43:34.570
retaliation in
academic settings.

00:43:34.570 --> 00:43:37.580
And this is a critically
important one, and one that,

00:43:37.580 --> 00:43:42.440
as Sheila mentioned earlier,
drives women, or drives

00:43:42.440 --> 00:43:46.200
targets, not to report.

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:50.400
Next, it's important
to improve transparency

00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:51.580
and accountability.

00:43:51.580 --> 00:43:53.430
Now, what do we mean by that?

00:43:53.430 --> 00:43:57.330
Academic institutions need
to develop and readily

00:43:57.330 --> 00:44:02.430
share clear, accessible,
and consistent policies

00:44:02.430 --> 00:44:06.940
on sexual harassment and
standards of behavior.

00:44:06.940 --> 00:44:09.900
They should include a
range of clearly stated

00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:13.620
appropriate and escalating
disciplinary consequences

00:44:13.620 --> 00:44:17.460
for perpetrators found to have
violated sexual harassment

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:20.280
policy and/or the law.

00:44:20.280 --> 00:44:23.100
Disciplinary action
should correspond

00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:27.030
to the severity and
frequency of the harassment.

00:44:27.030 --> 00:44:28.950
And the disciplinary
action should not

00:44:28.950 --> 00:44:31.500
be something that
is often considered

00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:36.381
a benefit for the faculty, such
as reduction in teaching load.

00:44:36.381 --> 00:44:38.970
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:38.970 --> 00:44:44.920
Or taking time away from campus
service responsibilities.

00:44:44.920 --> 00:44:47.350
Decisions regarding
disciplinary actions,

00:44:47.350 --> 00:44:50.080
if indicated or
required, should be

00:44:50.080 --> 00:44:53.890
made in a fair and
timely way, following

00:44:53.890 --> 00:44:59.050
an investigative process
that is fair to all sides.

00:44:59.050 --> 00:45:04.180
And academic institutions should
make as transparent as possible

00:45:04.180 --> 00:45:07.780
how they're handling reports
of sexual harassment.

00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:16.560
Next, we have to strive for
strong and diverse leadership.

00:45:16.560 --> 00:45:18.930
College and
university presidents,

00:45:18.930 --> 00:45:23.520
myself included, provosts,
deans, department chairs,

00:45:23.520 --> 00:45:28.230
and program directors must make
the reduction and prevention

00:45:28.230 --> 00:45:33.420
of sexual harassment an
explicit goal of their tenure.

00:45:33.420 --> 00:45:36.720
They should publicly state that
the reduction and prevention

00:45:36.720 --> 00:45:41.070
of sexual harassment will be
among their highest priorities,

00:45:41.070 --> 00:45:45.270
and they should engage
students, faculty, and staff,

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:48.990
and where appropriate, the local
community, in their efforts.

00:45:48.990 --> 00:45:50.970
And I do want to say,
President Reif, you

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:52.840
exemplify that leadership.

00:45:52.840 --> 00:45:54.274
Thank you.

00:45:54.274 --> 00:45:57.256
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:02.240 --> 00:46:05.410
And lastly, it's
important to make sure

00:46:05.410 --> 00:46:08.740
that the entire
academic community is

00:46:08.740 --> 00:46:13.540
responsible for reducing and
preventing sexual harassment.

00:46:13.540 --> 00:46:16.300
All members of our nation's
colleges and university

00:46:16.300 --> 00:46:20.930
campuses, including students,
trainees, faculty, staff,

00:46:20.930 --> 00:46:23.320
and administrators,
as well as members

00:46:23.320 --> 00:46:27.040
of research and training sites,
should assume responsibility

00:46:27.040 --> 00:46:31.690
for promoting civil and
respectful education, training,

00:46:31.690 --> 00:46:34.900
and work environments, and
stepping up and confronting

00:46:34.900 --> 00:46:37.690
those whose
behaviors and actions

00:46:37.690 --> 00:46:40.490
create sexually
harassing environments.

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:45.090
So next, I'm going
to go through some

00:46:45.090 --> 00:46:46.643
of the recommendations
for policy

00:46:46.643 --> 00:46:48.535
makers and federal agencies.

00:46:51.650 --> 00:46:57.140
These include one, better
protecting sexual harassment

00:46:57.140 --> 00:46:59.930
claimants from retaliation.

00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:04.580
Second, prohibiting
confidentiality

00:47:04.580 --> 00:47:07.520
in settlement agreements
that currently enable

00:47:07.520 --> 00:47:11.420
harassers to move to
another institution

00:47:11.420 --> 00:47:15.500
and conceal past adjudications.

00:47:15.500 --> 00:47:18.540
Banning mandatory
arbitration clauses--

00:47:18.540 --> 00:47:21.440
which by the fact that
they are arbitration

00:47:21.440 --> 00:47:23.330
means that they are private--

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:26.690
banning mandatory arbitration
clauses for discrimination

00:47:26.690 --> 00:47:28.482
claims.

00:47:28.482 --> 00:47:32.980
Allowing lawsuits to be filed
against alleged harassers

00:47:32.980 --> 00:47:35.980
directly, instead
of or in addition

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:38.350
to their academic employers.

00:47:40.880 --> 00:47:45.530
Requiring disclosure of
results from surveys.

00:47:45.530 --> 00:47:47.870
We should consider
requiring institutions

00:47:47.870 --> 00:47:51.650
receiving federal funding
to publicly disclose results

00:47:51.650 --> 00:47:56.600
from campus climate
surveys and/or the number

00:47:56.600 --> 00:48:02.110
of sexual harassment
reports made to campuses.

00:48:02.110 --> 00:48:03.940
We should also
consider requesting

00:48:03.940 --> 00:48:06.880
that the National Science
Foundation and the National

00:48:06.880 --> 00:48:10.480
Institutes of Health devote
research funds to doing

00:48:10.480 --> 00:48:14.860
follow-up analyses on the
topic of sexual harassment

00:48:14.860 --> 00:48:19.750
in science, engineering, and
medicine in three to five years

00:48:19.750 --> 00:48:22.150
to determine one,
whether research

00:48:22.150 --> 00:48:24.580
has shown that the prevalence
of sexual harassment

00:48:24.580 --> 00:48:25.900
has decreased.

00:48:25.900 --> 00:48:28.300
And second, whether
progress has been

00:48:28.300 --> 00:48:31.630
made on implementing
these recommendations.

00:48:31.630 --> 00:48:35.875
And third, how and where do
we focus our future efforts.

00:48:39.860 --> 00:48:43.510
We also have to attend
to sexual harassment

00:48:43.510 --> 00:48:47.620
with at least the same level
of attention and resources

00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:51.060
devoted to research misconduct.

00:48:51.060 --> 00:48:52.680
They should increase
collaboration

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:56.190
among offices that oversee
the integrity of research--

00:48:56.190 --> 00:48:58.470
for example, those
that cover ethics,

00:48:58.470 --> 00:49:02.700
research misconduct, diversity,
and harassment issues.

00:49:02.700 --> 00:49:07.320
Consider centralizing resources,
information, and expertise,

00:49:07.320 --> 00:49:10.710
providing more resources
for handling complaints

00:49:10.710 --> 00:49:12.600
and working with targets.

00:49:12.600 --> 00:49:15.570
And implementing
sanctions on researchers

00:49:15.570 --> 00:49:18.040
found guilty of
sexual harassment.

00:49:21.650 --> 00:49:25.250
Require institutions to
report to federal agencies

00:49:25.250 --> 00:49:27.740
when individuals
on grants have been

00:49:27.740 --> 00:49:31.700
found to have violated
sexual harassment policies

00:49:31.700 --> 00:49:34.910
or have been put on
administrative leave

00:49:34.910 --> 00:49:37.880
related to sexual harassment.

00:49:37.880 --> 00:49:42.050
This is a policy that the
National Science Foundation

00:49:42.050 --> 00:49:45.230
has proposed doing.

00:49:45.230 --> 00:49:48.590
Agencies should also hold
accountable the perpetrator

00:49:48.590 --> 00:49:53.930
and the institution by using a
range of disciplinary actions

00:49:53.930 --> 00:49:58.250
that limit the negative effects
on other grant personnel

00:49:58.250 --> 00:50:01.850
who were either the target
of the harassing behavior

00:50:01.850 --> 00:50:03.380
or innocent bystanders.

00:50:03.380 --> 00:50:05.690
And this is a very
important one in terms

00:50:05.690 --> 00:50:09.555
of not penalizing the entire
research team, for example.

00:50:12.630 --> 00:50:16.560
We should also increase support
for research and evaluation

00:50:16.560 --> 00:50:19.840
on the effectiveness of
policies, procedures,

00:50:19.840 --> 00:50:22.090
and training on
sexual harassment.

00:50:26.165 --> 00:50:30.320
And we should also
reward and incentivize

00:50:30.320 --> 00:50:32.810
colleges and universities
for implementing

00:50:32.810 --> 00:50:35.900
policies, and programs,
and strategies

00:50:35.900 --> 00:50:38.840
that research shows
are more likely to

00:50:38.840 --> 00:50:42.230
and are succeeding in
reducing and preventing

00:50:42.230 --> 00:50:45.930
sexual harassment.

00:50:45.930 --> 00:50:49.180
So those are our
recommendations.

00:50:49.180 --> 00:50:51.380
They are pretty broad.

00:50:51.380 --> 00:50:55.940
But we think that they are
actionable and can actually

00:50:55.940 --> 00:51:03.020
be put into place in some
reasonable period of time.

00:51:03.020 --> 00:51:07.430
The National Academies are
actually putting together

00:51:07.430 --> 00:51:11.360
a collaborative of
academic institutions

00:51:11.360 --> 00:51:15.320
to begin to better understand
what that implementation looks

00:51:15.320 --> 00:51:17.770
like and how we
can actually give

00:51:17.770 --> 00:51:20.640
feet and legs to this report.

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:21.748
So thank you.

00:51:21.748 --> 00:51:24.188
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:37.380 --> 00:51:42.240
Thank you, President Johnson
and Professor Widnall.

00:51:42.240 --> 00:51:48.160
Now is the time when we welcome
you to come to the microphones

00:51:48.160 --> 00:51:50.910
on either of these
aisles and ask

00:51:50.910 --> 00:51:53.610
your questions of the panel.

00:51:53.610 --> 00:51:55.230
I will take my seat.

00:51:55.230 --> 00:51:58.110
And please don't be shy.

00:51:58.110 --> 00:52:00.010
I'm sure many of you
do have questions.

00:52:00.010 --> 00:52:02.510
So here are the mics right here.

00:52:02.510 --> 00:52:04.360
And I welcome you to come down.

00:52:12.710 --> 00:52:13.210
Oh, it's on?

00:52:13.210 --> 00:52:14.110
Hi.

00:52:14.110 --> 00:52:16.120
So I'm David Singer
from political science.

00:52:16.120 --> 00:52:19.480
Thanks so much for being here
and sharing your thoughts

00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:23.500
in an incredibly detailed
and thought-provoking study.

00:52:23.500 --> 00:52:25.990
I have just a couple of
questions about that.

00:52:25.990 --> 00:52:29.140
One is that the
subtitle of your report

00:52:29.140 --> 00:52:33.100
is a Consensus Study, which
made me chuckle a little bit.

00:52:33.100 --> 00:52:35.740
Because when you get a group
of academics in a room,

00:52:35.740 --> 00:52:37.360
it's difficult to
come to consensus.

00:52:37.360 --> 00:52:38.140
So I'm curious.

00:52:38.140 --> 00:52:40.900
Where were the
dimensions of, maybe,

00:52:40.900 --> 00:52:43.780
constructive tension
among all of you

00:52:43.780 --> 00:52:46.080
when writing up this report?

00:52:46.080 --> 00:52:48.400
My second question is, what
are your recommendations

00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:53.350
for when students or faculty
are attending meetings

00:52:53.350 --> 00:52:55.330
run by academic
associations that

00:52:55.330 --> 00:52:57.640
are off campus and sort of
outside the jurisdiction

00:52:57.640 --> 00:52:58.850
of any particular university?

00:52:58.850 --> 00:52:59.350
Thanks.

00:53:03.380 --> 00:53:04.420
Yeah, why don't I--

00:53:04.420 --> 00:53:05.510
does this microphone work?

00:53:05.510 --> 00:53:06.830
Yes.

00:53:06.830 --> 00:53:09.590
OK.

00:53:09.590 --> 00:53:13.190
As I mentioned, the report
is a consensus report.

00:53:13.190 --> 00:53:15.960
And the committee
was very diverse.

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:18.080
We had basically
social scientists.

00:53:18.080 --> 00:53:20.160
We had engineers and scientists.

00:53:20.160 --> 00:53:22.590
We had a few people
from industry.

00:53:22.590 --> 00:53:25.310
We had college presidents.

00:53:25.310 --> 00:53:31.170
And we spent a lot of time
basically looking at the data,

00:53:31.170 --> 00:53:34.520
analyzing the data, trying
to come to consensus.

00:53:34.520 --> 00:53:37.340
I won't say there was yelling
and screaming across the table,

00:53:37.340 --> 00:53:39.480
but almost.

00:53:39.480 --> 00:53:42.860
And ultimately, we
all came to feel

00:53:42.860 --> 00:53:48.590
very comfortable with the
report as it was put together.

00:53:48.590 --> 00:53:50.930
Those of us who
had certain things

00:53:50.930 --> 00:53:52.610
we wanted to see happen--

00:53:52.610 --> 00:53:53.840
we were satisfied.

00:53:53.840 --> 00:53:57.110
And people who wanted to
say something about the data

00:53:57.110 --> 00:53:58.760
and how it should be acquired--

00:53:58.760 --> 00:53:59.930
I think they were satisfied.

00:53:59.930 --> 00:54:01.580
So I think in the
end, we-- but it

00:54:01.580 --> 00:54:04.930
took two years to kind
of come to an agreement.

00:54:04.930 --> 00:54:07.860
And I think it was a
very good piece of work.

00:54:07.860 --> 00:54:12.110
And I think that's what makes
the report strong is that--

00:54:12.110 --> 00:54:14.090
building that consensus.

00:54:14.090 --> 00:54:16.400
So do you want to say
something about the other?

00:54:16.400 --> 00:54:18.890
I want to just add
to what Sheila just

00:54:18.890 --> 00:54:22.880
said, which is that the
foundation of coming

00:54:22.880 --> 00:54:26.270
to consensus was evidence.

00:54:26.270 --> 00:54:28.610
And at the end of
the day, it is really

00:54:28.610 --> 00:54:33.380
the evidence that allowed
us to come to consensus.

00:54:33.380 --> 00:54:37.730
We are humans, and so we bring
to the table various opinions

00:54:37.730 --> 00:54:38.720
and views.

00:54:38.720 --> 00:54:42.380
The question is, are those
opinions and views always

00:54:42.380 --> 00:54:43.850
rooted in the best evidence?

00:54:43.850 --> 00:54:49.220
And we held ourselves extremely
accountable to that evidence.

00:54:49.220 --> 00:54:51.830
And that's why we
feel very strongly

00:54:51.830 --> 00:54:55.310
about the reports, the
recommendations-- the findings

00:54:55.310 --> 00:54:56.310
and the recommendations.

00:54:56.310 --> 00:54:57.680
This isn't opinion.

00:54:57.680 --> 00:54:59.810
It's really based on the data.

00:54:59.810 --> 00:55:02.170
For the second part of
the question regarding

00:55:02.170 --> 00:55:06.920
off-site activity, this is
a critically important area.

00:55:06.920 --> 00:55:09.860
And it's why we believe
that field sites

00:55:09.860 --> 00:55:11.660
are critically
important, and also

00:55:11.660 --> 00:55:13.680
professional organizations.

00:55:13.680 --> 00:55:15.290
A lot of the
professional societies

00:55:15.290 --> 00:55:20.540
are now taking on this issue and
creating a set of ground rules

00:55:20.540 --> 00:55:25.370
for what happens
at their meetings.

00:55:25.370 --> 00:55:29.730
So it's really an extension
of the academic institution.

00:55:29.730 --> 00:55:33.980
So I think we really need
to view those as a continuum

00:55:33.980 --> 00:55:36.830
and really work both at
the institutional level,

00:55:36.830 --> 00:55:40.290
but also for the
professional societies.

00:55:40.290 --> 00:55:42.470
Can I ask a question
about consensus?

00:55:42.470 --> 00:55:46.440
Because you made a
choice about language.

00:55:46.440 --> 00:55:48.440
And I think that's
often the hardest

00:55:48.440 --> 00:55:51.350
thing to get consensus around.

00:55:51.350 --> 00:55:56.360
One of the interesting
language choices that you made

00:55:56.360 --> 00:56:01.040
was about the use of
the term civility.

00:56:01.040 --> 00:56:03.800
And so I wonder if that
was a point of tension,

00:56:03.800 --> 00:56:09.290
or how you came to look
more at using civility

00:56:09.290 --> 00:56:14.330
as a sort of broader
category versus harassment,

00:56:14.330 --> 00:56:17.090
or some other kinds of phrases
that we might have used--

00:56:17.090 --> 00:56:20.480
abuse of power-- to focus on.

00:56:23.000 --> 00:56:25.890
You just mentioned--
civility is a positive word.

00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:27.720
Harassment is a negative word.

00:56:27.720 --> 00:56:31.320
So I think it's a lot easier
to choose the positive word,

00:56:31.320 --> 00:56:32.580
civility.

00:56:32.580 --> 00:56:33.760
I think it sells better.

00:56:39.280 --> 00:56:40.980
Anita, that's a great pickup.

00:56:40.980 --> 00:56:44.550
Because we did have
a lot of discussion.

00:56:44.550 --> 00:56:46.440
Because I think,
in some arenas, it

00:56:46.440 --> 00:56:48.312
can be viewed as a negative--

00:56:48.312 --> 00:56:49.770
even though it's
a positive, it can

00:56:49.770 --> 00:56:51.150
be viewed as a negative term.

00:56:51.150 --> 00:56:55.230
And it can be viewed
as somewhat term

00:56:55.230 --> 00:56:58.260
around how are we trying
to guide behavior.

00:56:58.260 --> 00:57:00.220
And I think when
we came down to it,

00:57:00.220 --> 00:57:03.630
what we're really talking
about here is civility,

00:57:03.630 --> 00:57:06.480
meaning a culture
of respect and how

00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:10.390
we behave towards each other.

00:57:10.390 --> 00:57:11.960
Over here.

00:57:11.960 --> 00:57:15.310
Thank you to all the panelists.

00:57:15.310 --> 00:57:17.650
My name is Rachel Kurchin,
and I'm a graduate student.

00:57:17.650 --> 00:57:20.590
And like, I think,
many PhD students,

00:57:20.590 --> 00:57:22.870
I've sort of bounced
around through my time

00:57:22.870 --> 00:57:24.730
through many different
funding sources.

00:57:24.730 --> 00:57:27.220
And so the point about
departmental funding

00:57:27.220 --> 00:57:29.930
as a way to diffuse the sort
of power relationship really

00:57:29.930 --> 00:57:30.430
struck me.

00:57:30.430 --> 00:57:31.805
And I think that's
something that

00:57:31.805 --> 00:57:34.690
would have a lot broader impact
than just diffusing that power

00:57:34.690 --> 00:57:36.970
relationship in terms of
people's sense of security

00:57:36.970 --> 00:57:41.545
and in terms of their funding
security, not personal safety,

00:57:41.545 --> 00:57:43.900
although it's that too.

00:57:43.900 --> 00:57:48.790
It strikes me, though, that that
could pose certain challenges

00:57:48.790 --> 00:57:51.640
to the way that research
is funded currently

00:57:51.640 --> 00:57:53.830
through the granting
agencies in terms

00:57:53.830 --> 00:57:56.710
of being granted to a group and
certain number of grad student

00:57:56.710 --> 00:57:57.980
years, or something like that.

00:57:57.980 --> 00:58:01.180
So I'm curious if the committee
talked about any changes

00:58:01.180 --> 00:58:04.480
in the sort of structure
or terms of actual research

00:58:04.480 --> 00:58:08.560
grants that might have to
be implemented to make such

00:58:08.560 --> 00:58:10.600
a proposal, which I would
be personally really

00:58:10.600 --> 00:58:13.690
excited about, kind of
feasible on a large scale,

00:58:13.690 --> 00:58:15.370
and not just to
institutions that

00:58:15.370 --> 00:58:17.680
sort of might have the
independent funding

00:58:17.680 --> 00:58:20.250
to make that happen.

00:58:20.250 --> 00:58:21.320
Well, you know, grants--

00:58:21.320 --> 00:58:23.690
I agree.

00:58:23.690 --> 00:58:28.050
This relationship
between mentor/mentee,

00:58:28.050 --> 00:58:34.520
PI/trainee, is so fundamental
to our research structure.

00:58:34.520 --> 00:58:37.700
But it also causes some of
the dynamics, as you said,

00:58:37.700 --> 00:58:41.360
that are highly problematic.

00:58:41.360 --> 00:58:44.600
Research grants are
granted to institutions.

00:58:44.600 --> 00:58:47.390
The PI is part of
the institution.

00:58:47.390 --> 00:58:49.322
So I'm not saying
that it's easy.

00:58:49.322 --> 00:58:50.780
I don't think we
believe it's easy.

00:58:50.780 --> 00:58:52.613
We actually didn't talk
about the specifics.

00:58:52.613 --> 00:58:55.760
But we felt that given the
money, that the money does

00:58:55.760 --> 00:58:58.040
come to institutions,
that there are

00:58:58.040 --> 00:59:03.430
ways to begin to
think about some

00:59:03.430 --> 00:59:07.630
of the grant money actually
being more centralized

00:59:07.630 --> 00:59:11.950
for the grantees when it
is allocated to grantees,

00:59:11.950 --> 00:59:17.848
as opposed to being dependent
on that one-to-one relationship.

00:59:17.848 --> 00:59:19.970
Thank you.

00:59:19.970 --> 00:59:20.470
Hi.

00:59:20.470 --> 00:59:21.220
Thank you so much.

00:59:21.220 --> 00:59:23.306
It couldn't be more timely.

00:59:23.306 --> 00:59:24.430
My name is Hilary Robinson.

00:59:24.430 --> 00:59:25.550
I was a student here.

00:59:25.550 --> 00:59:27.370
And I'm a professor
in the law school

00:59:27.370 --> 00:59:30.090
and the Department of
Sociology at Northeastern.

00:59:30.090 --> 00:59:31.787
I came late, so
forgive me if I'm

00:59:31.787 --> 00:59:33.370
going to reference
Catharine MacKinnon

00:59:33.370 --> 00:59:35.262
and you already spoke about her.

00:59:35.262 --> 00:59:37.720
But it was very notable to me
that the title of this report

00:59:37.720 --> 00:59:40.300
is "The Sexual Harassment of
Women," which is very evocative

00:59:40.300 --> 00:59:43.300
of MacKinnon's very
influential book, The Sexual

00:59:43.300 --> 00:59:45.340
Harassment of Working Women.

00:59:45.340 --> 00:59:49.690
And you mentioned, or in several
of your slides, Dr. Johnson,

00:59:49.690 --> 00:59:51.550
there was this
reference to the law

00:59:51.550 --> 00:59:54.340
and to the need for formal
legality in these kinds

00:59:54.340 --> 00:59:57.250
of proceedings, which is what
MacKinnon, who was also a law

00:59:57.250 --> 00:59:58.990
professor, called for.

00:59:58.990 --> 01:00:00.790
That until we think
about this as a kind

01:00:00.790 --> 01:00:03.940
of tort, which is what
the harassment word means,

01:00:03.940 --> 01:00:05.709
with all respect to
the idea of civility,

01:00:05.709 --> 01:00:07.750
being a kind of nicer way
to get people to engage

01:00:07.750 --> 01:00:09.129
in these conversations.

01:00:09.129 --> 01:00:11.170
And I just wonder if you
could speak specifically

01:00:11.170 --> 01:00:13.790
about your conversations
around the law.

01:00:13.790 --> 01:00:16.719
I know, Professor Hill,
you're a law professor.

01:00:16.719 --> 01:00:18.760
I think that would be
illuminating for all of us.

01:00:18.760 --> 01:00:20.260
Because sometimes
you really do need

01:00:20.260 --> 01:00:24.520
to push into the realm of
actual litigation, which

01:00:24.520 --> 01:00:27.310
requires money and
institutional support.

01:00:27.310 --> 01:00:28.760
Thank you.

01:00:28.760 --> 01:00:32.260
Well, I think one of
the things that we're

01:00:32.260 --> 01:00:37.510
learning in this moment
is that litigation

01:00:37.510 --> 01:00:44.110
has been a necessary but not
sufficient tool for eliminating

01:00:44.110 --> 01:00:48.470
the kind of harassment
that we know is prevalent.

01:00:48.470 --> 01:00:52.510
We also know that there are some
real limitations to litigation

01:00:52.510 --> 01:00:53.620
as a tool--

01:00:53.620 --> 01:00:58.780
access to law, access
to legal representation,

01:00:58.780 --> 01:01:00.830
is very limited.

01:01:00.830 --> 01:01:05.380
The legal system relies on
the most vulnerable people,

01:01:05.380 --> 01:01:08.260
very often, to
come and complain.

01:01:08.260 --> 01:01:10.600
And we know that there
are high levels--

01:01:10.600 --> 01:01:16.540
as much as 60% of people
will experience retaliation.

01:01:16.540 --> 01:01:22.450
And we also know that
for low income women,

01:01:22.450 --> 01:01:29.410
the amount of pay-off
in terms of wages

01:01:29.410 --> 01:01:33.460
often are not high enough
to support the finding

01:01:33.460 --> 01:01:35.860
of legal representation.

01:01:35.860 --> 01:01:38.860
So those are all of
the things that we're

01:01:38.860 --> 01:01:40.840
dealing with-- the
dynamics that we're

01:01:40.840 --> 01:01:45.070
dealing with-- that doesn't
make law irrelevant,

01:01:45.070 --> 01:01:50.210
but it makes it inadequate.

01:01:50.210 --> 01:01:54.160
And I believe that
our recommendations,

01:01:54.160 --> 01:01:57.580
the recommendations
of this volume,

01:01:57.580 --> 01:01:59.660
as well as
recommendations generally,

01:01:59.660 --> 01:02:04.420
are that yes, we need to have
some new legal strategies

01:02:04.420 --> 01:02:06.640
around sexual harassment.

01:02:06.640 --> 01:02:10.360
But we also need for
work to take place

01:02:10.360 --> 01:02:17.170
within institutions that does
not rely on even a complaint

01:02:17.170 --> 01:02:21.580
process, or a complaint getting
to the level of something that

01:02:21.580 --> 01:02:26.650
is to be litigated,
before we act.

01:02:26.650 --> 01:02:30.760
And I think I would not have
chosen the word civility

01:02:30.760 --> 01:02:33.964
because I'm a lawyer by
training, so I would--

01:02:33.964 --> 01:02:35.830
[LAUGHTER]

01:02:35.830 --> 01:02:40.960
But I think it is important
that we change the bar so

01:02:40.960 --> 01:02:44.920
that managers
appropriately trained

01:02:44.920 --> 01:02:49.270
can intervene before
things get to a level

01:02:49.270 --> 01:02:50.680
of a legal violation.

01:02:54.550 --> 01:02:57.820
Lawyers don't always work
well with other systems.

01:02:57.820 --> 01:03:02.830
But I think we've got
to work together on this

01:03:02.830 --> 01:03:06.610
and see this as a holistic,
systemic problem that

01:03:06.610 --> 01:03:11.542
will be resolved through a
lot of tactics and approaches.

01:03:11.542 --> 01:03:13.490
Thank you.

01:03:13.490 --> 01:03:16.420
As you mentioned, your
recommendations and definitions

01:03:16.420 --> 01:03:18.010
are pretty broad.

01:03:18.010 --> 01:03:20.650
For that very reason,
the Federal Department

01:03:20.650 --> 01:03:23.260
of Education, last
year, rescinded

01:03:23.260 --> 01:03:29.590
that infamous April 2011 "Dear
Colleagues" letter in order

01:03:29.590 --> 01:03:33.520
to better protect the rights
and interests of the accused.

01:03:33.520 --> 01:03:35.320
Now, I haven't heard
you say anything

01:03:35.320 --> 01:03:38.530
about how you would protect
the rights of the accused,

01:03:38.530 --> 01:03:40.560
free speech, and due process.

01:03:40.560 --> 01:03:43.470
So you consider those to
be antiquated concepts now.

01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:48.970
In my presentation on
the recommendations,

01:03:48.970 --> 01:03:55.750
I actually very clearly stated
that processes that were clear

01:03:55.750 --> 01:03:57.940
and were fair to all--

01:03:57.940 --> 01:04:02.200
both the accused
and the accuser--

01:04:02.200 --> 01:04:06.160
should be implemented, and that
there should be transparency

01:04:06.160 --> 01:04:08.530
of those, and that
we should also

01:04:08.530 --> 01:04:13.900
consider very clear
guidance around what is it

01:04:13.900 --> 01:04:16.160
that will result from
certain behavior.

01:04:16.160 --> 01:04:18.230
So this is not a black box.

01:04:18.230 --> 01:04:20.290
So if that wasn't
clear, I just want

01:04:20.290 --> 01:04:22.330
to really make that very clear.

01:04:22.330 --> 01:04:27.280
Fair processes are
very important for all.

01:04:30.600 --> 01:04:31.244
Thank you.

01:04:31.244 --> 01:04:31.910
I'm Judy Singer.

01:04:31.910 --> 01:04:34.035
I'm the Senior Vice Provost
for Faculty Development

01:04:34.035 --> 01:04:36.270
and Diversity at
Harvard, down the street.

01:04:36.270 --> 01:04:39.270
And I want to focus on the
subtitle of your report

01:04:39.270 --> 01:04:41.520
and ask a question about
your deliberations--

01:04:41.520 --> 01:04:43.560
"Climate, Culture
and Consequences."

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:45.880
And I'll just
abbreviate to academia.

01:04:45.880 --> 01:04:49.110
So one of the questions that
I've been having conversations

01:04:49.110 --> 01:04:53.850
with people around is the Me
Too movement, this report,

01:04:53.850 --> 01:04:56.700
bring to light issues
of sexual harassment,

01:04:56.700 --> 01:04:59.310
generally, and also
in the academy.

01:04:59.310 --> 01:05:02.070
But we're also concerned about--

01:05:02.070 --> 01:05:05.190
perhaps it's the flip word of
civility, but I'll use the word

01:05:05.190 --> 01:05:06.000
that's used--

01:05:06.000 --> 01:05:10.290
bullying and the concern
that in a lot of our academic

01:05:10.290 --> 01:05:12.770
settings-- while there
may be sexual harassment--

01:05:12.770 --> 01:05:14.940
and I'm not making light of it--

01:05:14.940 --> 01:05:17.070
there is also bullying.

01:05:17.070 --> 01:05:17.790
What is it?

01:05:17.790 --> 01:05:18.630
Bullying.

01:05:18.630 --> 01:05:25.010
Bullying-- both in terms
of trainee and supervisor

01:05:25.010 --> 01:05:28.950
relationships, but also
collegial relationships, staff,

01:05:28.950 --> 01:05:30.240
and faculty relationships.

01:05:30.240 --> 01:05:32.460
I could go on, but I
think you get the context.

01:05:32.460 --> 01:05:36.180
Did this come up at all
in your deliberations

01:05:36.180 --> 01:05:39.720
as another issue
to take on, which

01:05:39.720 --> 01:05:43.530
is related to but different
from the whole question

01:05:43.530 --> 01:05:46.110
of sexual harassment?

01:05:46.110 --> 01:05:48.585
You're absolutely right, Judy.

01:05:48.585 --> 01:05:51.390
And this report
is not meant to be

01:05:51.390 --> 01:05:54.540
a full kind of
comprehensive report

01:05:54.540 --> 01:05:56.700
on all types of harassment.

01:05:56.700 --> 01:05:59.190
We were really focused
on a particular charge

01:05:59.190 --> 01:06:01.020
around sexual harassment.

01:06:01.020 --> 01:06:06.430
But absolutely, there are
other forms of harassment.

01:06:06.430 --> 01:06:07.690
So you're absolutely right.

01:06:07.690 --> 01:06:10.880
I think it's another
body of work to be done.

01:06:10.880 --> 01:06:13.470
And it's worthwhile, actually,
making another statement.

01:06:13.470 --> 01:06:16.140
We were asked very
specifically to look

01:06:16.140 --> 01:06:18.610
at sexual harassment in women.

01:06:18.610 --> 01:06:23.880
So I want to make very clear
that sexual minorities--

01:06:23.880 --> 01:06:30.330
nonconforming people-- actually
experience sexual harassment

01:06:30.330 --> 01:06:34.680
at rates that are either
at the same level,

01:06:34.680 --> 01:06:37.920
or around the same
level, of women of color,

01:06:37.920 --> 01:06:40.420
racial, and ethnic minorities.

01:06:40.420 --> 01:06:43.500
So again, this report--

01:06:43.500 --> 01:06:48.240
we were given a very specific
charge and focused on that.

01:06:48.240 --> 01:06:50.513
But the other areas are
critically important.

01:06:50.513 --> 01:06:53.620
Thank you.

01:06:53.620 --> 01:06:54.150
Hi.

01:06:54.150 --> 01:06:56.670
I am [? Ona ?] [? Hidari. ?]
I am a research fellow at MIT.

01:06:56.670 --> 01:06:57.810
But I'm also a journalist.

01:06:57.810 --> 01:07:01.290
So my question is going to
be slightly more general

01:07:01.290 --> 01:07:02.887
for Ms. Hill.

01:07:02.887 --> 01:07:05.220
I've only spent three weeks
in the United States so far.

01:07:05.220 --> 01:07:10.116
And the topic of discussion
the past couple of days,

01:07:10.116 --> 01:07:11.490
as many of you
know, is something

01:07:11.490 --> 01:07:15.060
that relates to what you're
doing, but on a wider scale.

01:07:15.060 --> 01:07:19.789
How do you feel that the
conclusions of your report

01:07:19.789 --> 01:07:22.330
can be promoted institutions of
higher learning in the United

01:07:22.330 --> 01:07:24.785
States if the media scene has--

01:07:24.785 --> 01:07:26.410
some certain parts
of the media scene--

01:07:26.410 --> 01:07:28.800
have an apologetic
approach towards what

01:07:28.800 --> 01:07:32.460
is the recent findings in
relation to Judge Kavanaugh?

01:07:32.460 --> 01:07:36.210
And to Ms. Hill, will you be
watching the hearing on Monday

01:07:36.210 --> 01:07:36.955
anywhere?

01:07:36.955 --> 01:07:39.805
[LAUGHTER]

01:07:42.460 --> 01:07:48.850
On Monday, I will be in a
session most of the day,

01:07:48.850 --> 01:07:52.470
and not in Washington
in a session.

01:07:52.470 --> 01:07:57.180
But I will tune in as I can.

01:07:57.180 --> 01:08:00.990
And I guess I'm not quite
clear on your first question.

01:08:00.990 --> 01:08:02.640
How can the findings
of this report

01:08:02.640 --> 01:08:05.250
be promoted and also
accepted by institutions

01:08:05.250 --> 01:08:07.890
of higher learning,
and if there are

01:08:07.890 --> 01:08:09.630
certain segments
of the population,

01:08:09.630 --> 01:08:11.580
but also especially
the media scene, who

01:08:11.580 --> 01:08:18.310
aren't as critical of sexual
harassment as they should be,

01:08:18.310 --> 01:08:21.580
as was witnessed in the coverage
of the Kavanaugh hearing.

01:08:21.580 --> 01:08:25.529
Well, I think that the
promotion of this report

01:08:25.529 --> 01:08:27.350
has already started.

01:08:27.350 --> 01:08:32.520
And honestly, one of the
first days that it was out,

01:08:32.520 --> 01:08:34.710
I actually talked
to a journalist.

01:08:34.710 --> 01:08:38.010
And they were really
pleased with this report,

01:08:38.010 --> 01:08:42.430
specifically because it gives
concrete recommendations.

01:08:42.430 --> 01:08:49.560
And I think that's going to
be the best way to promote it,

01:08:49.560 --> 01:08:53.220
to show that there are concrete
actions that can be taken.

01:08:55.979 --> 01:08:58.710
And I don't want to advise
anybody on their promotion.

01:08:58.710 --> 01:09:06.210
But I will just say,
leading with the specific

01:09:06.210 --> 01:09:10.920
recommendation is, I
think, an effective way

01:09:10.920 --> 01:09:14.100
to communicate that
this is serious work

01:09:14.100 --> 01:09:16.151
and that it can have
real consequences.

01:09:16.151 --> 01:09:17.859
Because that's what
people are ready for.

01:09:20.080 --> 01:09:20.580
Hi.

01:09:20.580 --> 01:09:21.960
My name is Chen.

01:09:21.960 --> 01:09:25.870
And actually, I came here
to speak for another--

01:09:25.870 --> 01:09:29.520
I'm the speaker, and
intentionally strategize

01:09:29.520 --> 01:09:31.689
so that I could be here.

01:09:31.689 --> 01:09:34.620
Because it's an honor
to meet Anita Hill.

01:09:34.620 --> 01:09:36.569
But also because I
work on this issue

01:09:36.569 --> 01:09:40.990
globally for millennial
womens around the world.

01:09:40.990 --> 01:09:43.120
And since this report--

01:09:43.120 --> 01:09:45.540
I mean, the person
who was before me--

01:09:45.540 --> 01:09:49.439
you said you were charged
with the specific question.

01:09:49.439 --> 01:09:51.390
So it answered some
of my question.

01:09:51.390 --> 01:09:54.060
But I'm actually concerned
more with the title.

01:09:54.060 --> 01:09:56.240
Because globally,
I see this problem.

01:09:56.240 --> 01:10:01.350
It is rampant, and it is
unbelievable and unimaginable.

01:10:01.350 --> 01:10:03.840
And I really believe we
need to change that title

01:10:03.840 --> 01:10:07.140
to "sexual harassment by men."

01:10:07.140 --> 01:10:10.230
Because if we are really
talking about accountability,

01:10:10.230 --> 01:10:11.850
and we're talking about--

01:10:11.850 --> 01:10:13.740
I don't understand why--

01:10:13.740 --> 01:10:17.520
to me, by putting "women,"
the onus is on us.

01:10:17.520 --> 01:10:19.860
And globally, young
women around the world

01:10:19.860 --> 01:10:22.500
are screaming, why
do we keep focusing

01:10:22.500 --> 01:10:28.212
on us and not the perpetrator?

01:10:28.212 --> 01:10:31.104
[APPLAUSE]

01:10:37.230 --> 01:10:40.020
Let me say something about that.

01:10:40.020 --> 01:10:43.080
I think it was important
to have a title that

01:10:43.080 --> 01:10:45.010
is as broad as possible.

01:10:45.010 --> 01:10:48.450
And that's why I do like
the "Climate Culture."

01:10:48.450 --> 01:10:50.610
Because it basically
says it isn't

01:10:50.610 --> 01:10:54.750
just a few outrageous acts.

01:10:54.750 --> 01:10:58.500
It is the entire
climate and culture

01:10:58.500 --> 01:11:01.470
that women find themselves in.

01:11:01.470 --> 01:11:07.320
So I really felt that we needed
to broaden the task that we

01:11:07.320 --> 01:11:13.530
were given, rather than just
focusing on small things.

01:11:13.530 --> 01:11:16.380
And I'm just going
to say, the focus

01:11:16.380 --> 01:11:20.880
was on sexual harassment
of women, predominantly.

01:11:20.880 --> 01:11:23.010
And we say it clearly
in the report,

01:11:23.010 --> 01:11:26.646
which is predominantly by men.

01:11:26.646 --> 01:11:28.020
I mean, just
technically, that is

01:11:28.020 --> 01:11:30.400
what we were asked to focus on.

01:11:30.400 --> 01:11:32.820
If you say, sexual
harassment by men,

01:11:32.820 --> 01:11:35.040
that can be of women,
which is the majority.

01:11:35.040 --> 01:11:38.130
It can be but,
you know, for men.

01:11:38.130 --> 01:11:39.630
I mean, so I think it wasn't--

01:11:39.630 --> 01:11:43.050
I get your point, and it's
a very important point.

01:11:43.050 --> 01:11:46.710
And we do focus in the
report on the issue

01:11:46.710 --> 01:11:50.160
that this is predominantly
male on female.

01:11:50.160 --> 01:11:54.090
But technically, the title
was really the right title

01:11:54.090 --> 01:11:55.520
for the charge we were given.

01:12:01.081 --> 01:12:01.580
Hi.

01:12:01.580 --> 01:12:02.780
My name is Emily Neal.

01:12:02.780 --> 01:12:05.570
I run the Women's and
Gender Studies program here.

01:12:05.570 --> 01:12:06.850
And I was curious--

01:12:06.850 --> 01:12:08.900
I thought I heard you
say, in the beginning

01:12:08.900 --> 01:12:12.080
of your recommendations,
that you specifically

01:12:12.080 --> 01:12:14.510
recommended that sexual
harassment training be

01:12:14.510 --> 01:12:16.120
done in person.

01:12:16.120 --> 01:12:18.650
Is that what you said?

01:12:18.650 --> 01:12:22.320
It's one of the recommendations
in the early data.

01:12:22.320 --> 01:12:24.830
The data that we
have to date actually

01:12:24.830 --> 01:12:30.400
suggests that in-person
training is more effective.

01:12:30.400 --> 01:12:34.490
Were there any-- I guess
in terms of that research,

01:12:34.490 --> 01:12:37.610
do large institutions
have examples

01:12:37.610 --> 01:12:41.470
of doing in-person training
for large populations?

01:12:41.470 --> 01:12:43.700
So if this is one of
our recommendations,

01:12:43.700 --> 01:12:46.130
actually, that this is
an area, we actually

01:12:46.130 --> 01:12:49.130
tried our best to stay away
from recommendations that

01:12:49.130 --> 01:12:51.080
said we need further study.

01:12:51.080 --> 01:12:54.410
Because there's nothing
like a report that just says

01:12:54.410 --> 01:12:56.970
you need to study it more.

01:12:56.970 --> 01:13:03.500
But this is an area where there
really is inadequate data.

01:13:03.500 --> 01:13:07.790
And the limited data available
does suggest those things

01:13:07.790 --> 01:13:10.160
that I stated,
which are in person,

01:13:10.160 --> 01:13:13.460
of a particular length,
targeted specifically

01:13:13.460 --> 01:13:16.970
towards different groups,
having leadership present

01:13:16.970 --> 01:13:20.570
and focused on behavior change,
and not on changing beliefs,

01:13:20.570 --> 01:13:23.060
that those were the
characteristics.

01:13:23.060 --> 01:13:27.440
But this is really an area
that requires more evaluation

01:13:27.440 --> 01:13:29.420
in order to understand,
for example,

01:13:29.420 --> 01:13:34.350
in an institution of this size,
what would that look like.

01:13:34.350 --> 01:13:36.160
So I think there's
work to be done.

01:13:36.160 --> 01:13:36.660
Thanks.

01:13:36.660 --> 01:13:38.380
And thanks for the op-ed today.

01:13:41.200 --> 01:13:42.610
Hi.

01:13:42.610 --> 01:13:45.410
First of all, thank you all.

01:13:45.410 --> 01:13:46.750
My name is Melinda Deramo.

01:13:46.750 --> 01:13:48.291
And I was just
wondering if you would

01:13:48.291 --> 01:13:50.640
have advice for
something, like something

01:13:50.640 --> 01:13:56.340
I could do, to get visibility
into an institution,

01:13:56.340 --> 01:13:57.349
a laboratory--

01:13:57.349 --> 01:13:58.390
I work for a laboratory--

01:13:58.390 --> 01:14:01.030
4,000 people, 13% women.

01:14:01.030 --> 01:14:06.460
And just visibility into the
harassment cases, numbers.

01:14:06.460 --> 01:14:09.310
And the organization
is very adamant

01:14:09.310 --> 01:14:12.430
that it should be handled only
on a case-by-case basis, where

01:14:12.430 --> 01:14:15.520
there's just no awareness.

01:14:15.520 --> 01:14:17.350
Anything-- advice,
like anything I

01:14:17.350 --> 01:14:20.290
could lean on to
try to get a survey,

01:14:20.290 --> 01:14:23.590
or like when that's not allowed?

01:14:23.590 --> 01:14:26.320
I assume from what you say
that you're a graduate student.

01:14:26.320 --> 01:14:27.290
Is that correct?

01:14:27.290 --> 01:14:27.790
No.

01:14:27.790 --> 01:14:28.300
I work.

01:14:28.300 --> 01:14:29.761
Oh, you work.

01:14:29.761 --> 01:14:30.723
Yeah.

01:14:30.723 --> 01:14:33.609
[LAUGHTER]

01:14:39.470 --> 01:14:42.410
I guess what I would recommend--

01:14:42.410 --> 01:14:43.970
I was a graduate student.

01:14:43.970 --> 01:14:46.940
I have been very impressed
with the Association

01:14:46.940 --> 01:14:49.850
of Women Graduate Students
and the Association

01:14:49.850 --> 01:14:51.090
of Undergraduates.

01:14:51.090 --> 01:14:54.500
And I think various
groups can form.

01:14:54.500 --> 01:14:58.550
People can sort of form
groups, and join, and discuss.

01:14:58.550 --> 01:15:02.430
And that group
becomes supportive,

01:15:02.430 --> 01:15:04.460
and it becomes effective.

01:15:04.460 --> 01:15:08.110
And you can move
the issue along.

01:15:08.110 --> 01:15:14.000
And it gets the people involved,
and they will learn more.

01:15:14.000 --> 01:15:16.850
So I would recommend
forming some groups,

01:15:16.850 --> 01:15:21.500
and talking about it, and trying
to move the ball down the road.

01:15:21.500 --> 01:15:22.373
We have been.

01:15:22.373 --> 01:15:23.702
Yeah.

01:15:23.702 --> 01:15:25.480
OK, thanks.

01:15:25.480 --> 01:15:26.980
Hi, I'm Mike Bono.

01:15:26.980 --> 01:15:28.400
And I'm a post-doc here.

01:15:28.400 --> 01:15:30.700
And I want to thank you all
for coming and presenting.

01:15:30.700 --> 01:15:33.220
So my question is, I remember
when I was in grad school,

01:15:33.220 --> 01:15:35.740
there was this clear and
pervasive message of, if you

01:15:35.740 --> 01:15:38.320
crossed a professor
directly-- whether it was

01:15:38.320 --> 01:15:40.270
your advisor, or
another professor,

01:15:40.270 --> 01:15:42.485
or you were the
victim of harassment,

01:15:42.485 --> 01:15:44.110
or you were just a
bystander-- that you

01:15:44.110 --> 01:15:48.160
crossed a professor directly,
they would ruin your career.

01:15:48.160 --> 01:15:50.890
And I think that we have
this challenge in academia,

01:15:50.890 --> 01:15:53.110
in particular with this
idea of a good word

01:15:53.110 --> 01:15:55.274
from the right person
can make your career

01:15:55.274 --> 01:15:56.440
and a bad word can break it.

01:15:56.440 --> 01:15:59.280
So my question is, as
researchers, and as

01:15:59.280 --> 01:16:02.887
a research community, how
can we change this culture

01:16:02.887 --> 01:16:04.720
to make it easier for
people to come forward

01:16:04.720 --> 01:16:08.140
in cases of harassment?

01:16:08.140 --> 01:16:11.440
Well, I mean, this
is at the heart

01:16:11.440 --> 01:16:13.870
of some of the
recommendations, which

01:16:13.870 --> 01:16:18.850
is the fear of retaliation.

01:16:18.850 --> 01:16:23.240
And until we really begin
to change the culture,

01:16:23.240 --> 01:16:25.380
this is not going
to be a one by each.

01:16:25.380 --> 01:16:27.140
Because that's not
the way to change it.

01:16:27.140 --> 01:16:29.290
And that actually has
been our approach.

01:16:29.290 --> 01:16:34.390
We get a bad actor and a person
who has violated the policy.

01:16:34.390 --> 01:16:36.200
And then, OK, we deal with that.

01:16:36.200 --> 01:16:38.380
And then it's over
until the next one.

01:16:38.380 --> 01:16:41.860
So it's really not until
the culture changes.

01:16:41.860 --> 01:16:44.090
I'm not saying you don't
have to do those things.

01:16:44.090 --> 01:16:47.320
But it's not really until
the culture changes that we

01:16:47.320 --> 01:16:50.320
begin to change the behavior.

01:16:50.320 --> 01:16:53.710
And this issue about the fear
of retaliation is a real one.

01:16:53.710 --> 01:16:58.300
And I think that that's one
that, again, from the very top,

01:16:58.300 --> 01:17:01.330
all the way down to
colleagues-- and it's

01:17:01.330 --> 01:17:03.220
going to take
colleague-to-colleague pressure

01:17:03.220 --> 01:17:06.160
as well, and responsibility.

01:17:06.160 --> 01:17:07.720
And that's the
way I really think

01:17:07.720 --> 01:17:11.530
we're going to begin to change
the culture so that there isn't

01:17:11.530 --> 01:17:12.910
this fear.

01:17:12.910 --> 01:17:16.510
And to think about, again,
diffusing that power

01:17:16.510 --> 01:17:20.710
relationship, which is when
you are fully dependent

01:17:20.710 --> 01:17:22.760
on one person--

01:17:22.760 --> 01:17:24.610
and you know, that can
be either in a class,

01:17:24.610 --> 01:17:26.740
obviously that's one
way, but usually it's

01:17:26.740 --> 01:17:29.740
in a research environment--

01:17:29.740 --> 01:17:31.610
that can be highly problematic.

01:17:31.610 --> 01:17:36.580
So who are the others around
you in some network of mentors

01:17:36.580 --> 01:17:44.160
that you can rely on to help
in these situations, as well

01:17:44.160 --> 01:17:45.310
as the institution.

01:17:45.310 --> 01:17:47.680
Just form a community.

01:17:47.680 --> 01:17:51.640
Talk to other professors,
talk to other students.

01:17:51.640 --> 01:17:53.380
Go to the department head.

01:17:53.380 --> 01:17:58.540
Share information with people
who, in a fundamental way,

01:17:58.540 --> 01:18:01.180
share your values.

01:18:01.180 --> 01:18:05.050
But I have to say, I really
do understand your question.

01:18:05.050 --> 01:18:09.100
Because I've been on
committees, hiring committees,

01:18:09.100 --> 01:18:12.370
and we get letters
of recommendation.

01:18:12.370 --> 01:18:15.400
And we look at the letters,
and we say, oh, this person

01:18:15.400 --> 01:18:16.330
studied with x.

01:18:16.330 --> 01:18:20.200
Why isn't there a letter
from x in the file?

01:18:20.200 --> 01:18:22.720
And retaliation can be overt.

01:18:22.720 --> 01:18:23.545
It can be covert.

01:18:26.350 --> 01:18:32.080
And so what we have to do is
not only question the power

01:18:32.080 --> 01:18:35.110
or diffuse it, but we've
got to question our systems

01:18:35.110 --> 01:18:39.880
for evaluating people
for jobs, whether or not

01:18:39.880 --> 01:18:42.700
we are opening up
the door, really,

01:18:42.700 --> 01:18:49.150
for this kind of behavior
that allows for, or enables,

01:18:49.150 --> 01:18:51.820
those kinds of power abuses.

01:18:51.820 --> 01:18:55.990
And so I think that it's
more than just burden

01:18:55.990 --> 01:18:57.700
on the students.

01:18:57.700 --> 01:19:01.120
We have to think about
how the dynamics work

01:19:01.120 --> 01:19:05.500
and whether or not, in our
hiring processes and decisions,

01:19:05.500 --> 01:19:08.160
we are contributing to it.

01:19:08.160 --> 01:19:11.250
And I do think that the
leadership can be very helpful.

01:19:11.250 --> 01:19:12.070
And by no means--

01:19:12.070 --> 01:19:14.230
I agree with you, Anita--

01:19:14.230 --> 01:19:16.300
this is not the
job of the student.

01:19:16.300 --> 01:19:20.400
It is the job of leadership
and leadership at all levels.

01:19:20.400 --> 01:19:25.290
And this point about
how we make decisions

01:19:25.290 --> 01:19:29.310
is critically important,
in addition to, again,

01:19:29.310 --> 01:19:33.330
getting to when we're hiring--

01:19:33.330 --> 01:19:38.280
this idea of the way
that we protect harassers

01:19:38.280 --> 01:19:42.000
with various confidentiality
agreements, again,

01:19:42.000 --> 01:19:45.780
and how we pass people from
institution to institution.

01:19:45.780 --> 01:19:47.025
We really do have to stop.

01:19:51.530 --> 01:19:52.105
Hi.

01:19:52.105 --> 01:19:52.730
My name's Kara.

01:19:52.730 --> 01:19:53.690
I'm a graduate student.

01:19:53.690 --> 01:19:55.430
And thank you guys so much.

01:19:55.430 --> 01:19:57.800
So the report-- in what you
talked about today, there's

01:19:57.800 --> 01:20:00.620
a lot of discussion about
diagnosing the problem

01:20:00.620 --> 01:20:03.470
and recommendations
for fixing the problem.

01:20:03.470 --> 01:20:05.360
But to me, a lot
of it feels reliant

01:20:05.360 --> 01:20:08.750
on getting people to
care in the first place

01:20:08.750 --> 01:20:10.820
and want to participate
in these things.

01:20:10.820 --> 01:20:13.911
So I mean, just
like two examples.

01:20:13.911 --> 01:20:15.410
At the highest
levels, I think a lot

01:20:15.410 --> 01:20:17.480
of people in this
room probably, maybe,

01:20:17.480 --> 01:20:18.871
saw the documentary on Netflix.

01:20:18.871 --> 01:20:20.120
I hope this is the right name.

01:20:20.120 --> 01:20:22.310
It's called Hunting
Ground, which

01:20:22.310 --> 01:20:23.930
I think was really
shocking, that

01:20:23.930 --> 01:20:26.150
showed kind of
the extreme levels

01:20:26.150 --> 01:20:30.560
that universities go to to
cover these-- you know, in some

01:20:30.560 --> 01:20:33.920
of these more severe
sexual assault cases,

01:20:33.920 --> 01:20:35.750
so that they don't
ever get to light.

01:20:35.750 --> 01:20:40.340
And then on the lowest level,
on a peer-to-peer level,

01:20:40.340 --> 01:20:42.710
I think there's like a lot
of women in this room--

01:20:42.710 --> 01:20:44.730
probably more women
than men it seems like--

01:20:44.730 --> 01:20:46.550
and I don't think
women outnumber

01:20:46.550 --> 01:20:47.840
men in this university.

01:20:47.840 --> 01:20:51.260
And I think with my own
personal experiences,

01:20:51.260 --> 01:20:53.060
I'm involved in a lot
of personal things

01:20:53.060 --> 01:20:55.040
with trying to
start, you know, more

01:20:55.040 --> 01:20:58.640
at grass roots or whatever,
movements to support women,

01:20:58.640 --> 01:21:00.350
but also support
students in general.

01:21:00.350 --> 01:21:02.630
And it seems like in my
experience with that,

01:21:02.630 --> 01:21:04.000
it's also women-dominated.

01:21:04.000 --> 01:21:06.554
So how do you get
people, my peers,

01:21:06.554 --> 01:21:07.970
that are men at
the lowest levels,

01:21:07.970 --> 01:21:10.619
and how do you get universities
at the highest levels

01:21:10.619 --> 01:21:13.160
to care about this, and where
does that fit into this report?

01:21:13.160 --> 01:21:16.730
So I'm going to begin by
the highest levels, which

01:21:16.730 --> 01:21:21.230
is part of what we are doing
with this report is really

01:21:21.230 --> 01:21:25.370
focusing on the highest
level university, college

01:21:25.370 --> 01:21:29.130
presidents and others
in positions of power.

01:21:29.130 --> 01:21:30.830
How do we get this
word out to them?

01:21:30.830 --> 01:21:34.940
And then, how do we get them to
start working with each other?

01:21:34.940 --> 01:21:38.210
Because there's nothing
like peer pressure.

01:21:38.210 --> 01:21:41.690
This has really got to
come from those in power.

01:21:41.690 --> 01:21:44.430
It's not the responsibility--

01:21:44.430 --> 01:21:48.070
I mean, obviously, at the lowest
level, there's work to be done.

01:21:48.070 --> 01:21:52.310
But we have got to get
the highest level engaged.

01:21:52.310 --> 01:21:54.270
The organizations that
need to be engaged.

01:21:54.270 --> 01:21:58.670
I don't know how many people
saw that the triple AS--

01:21:58.670 --> 01:22:01.100
Peggy Hamburg is the
president-- they just

01:22:01.100 --> 01:22:06.950
announced that they will
actually take action

01:22:06.950 --> 01:22:11.810
to remove members who have
been found in violation

01:22:11.810 --> 01:22:14.660
of sexual harassment policies.

01:22:14.660 --> 01:22:18.440
And the National Academies
of Science. engineering,

01:22:18.440 --> 01:22:22.310
and Medicine are
looking at this as well.

01:22:22.310 --> 01:22:25.220
I think that this is
the kind of change

01:22:25.220 --> 01:22:28.700
that we really need
to begin seeing.

01:22:28.700 --> 01:22:31.520
Francis Collins came out with
a statement, either yesterday

01:22:31.520 --> 01:22:35.600
or two days ago, about
harassment at the NIH.

01:22:35.600 --> 01:22:37.620
It's a good statement--
not strong enough.

01:22:37.620 --> 01:22:39.600
But it's a step in
the right direction.

01:22:39.600 --> 01:22:42.470
So I think there is the
National Science Foundation

01:22:42.470 --> 01:22:43.980
and what it plans.

01:22:43.980 --> 01:22:49.640
There's got to be a movement at
the top around changing the way

01:22:49.640 --> 01:22:52.070
we do business to make
some of the things

01:22:52.070 --> 01:22:54.740
that you are discussing--

01:22:54.740 --> 01:22:59.360
quite frankly, not only are
they illegal to, you know,

01:22:59.360 --> 01:23:04.580
cover up that kind of action,
but just generally not done

01:23:04.580 --> 01:23:05.870
in institutions.

01:23:09.361 --> 01:23:09.860
Hi.

01:23:09.860 --> 01:23:10.901
My name's Elizabeth Burr.

01:23:10.901 --> 01:23:14.120
I'm one of the
undergrads here at MIT.

01:23:14.120 --> 01:23:17.540
And I believe what you guys
said about protecting people

01:23:17.540 --> 01:23:20.690
from retaliation, or removing
the fear of retaliation,

01:23:20.690 --> 01:23:23.300
would make a
phenomenal difference.

01:23:23.300 --> 01:23:24.450
Did you guys put--

01:23:24.450 --> 01:23:29.150
or what thought or
ideas did you have

01:23:29.150 --> 01:23:32.930
about protecting people
in such a social media

01:23:32.930 --> 01:23:36.380
heavy environment?

01:23:36.380 --> 01:23:39.180
So one, it's a very good point.

01:23:39.180 --> 01:23:41.510
And I may not answer
your question directly.

01:23:41.510 --> 01:23:44.300
But I want to give you
an example of where

01:23:44.300 --> 01:23:49.100
it's very clear women
do fear retaliation

01:23:49.100 --> 01:23:51.590
and often will not report.

01:23:51.590 --> 01:23:57.920
What mechanisms can
we use to actually get

01:23:57.920 --> 01:24:02.960
women to identify the
instances of harassment that

01:24:02.960 --> 01:24:06.290
have occurred, without actually
coming forward until they

01:24:06.290 --> 01:24:07.710
are ready to come forward?

01:24:07.710 --> 01:24:13.730
So there, for example, is
a program called Callisto.

01:24:13.730 --> 01:24:18.140
And it was started
out on the west coast.

01:24:18.140 --> 01:24:21.020
Now a number of colleges
and universities use it.

01:24:21.020 --> 01:24:23.450
And what it is, it's
the reporting system

01:24:23.450 --> 01:24:29.060
that allows a target to go
in and actually time stamp

01:24:29.060 --> 01:24:33.470
and identify an incident--
and can do it anonymously.

01:24:33.470 --> 01:24:35.990
And you can actually
have access to data

01:24:35.990 --> 01:24:40.850
to understand whether or not
there are others reporting.

01:24:40.850 --> 01:24:44.090
And it is there in
the record so that,

01:24:44.090 --> 01:24:48.900
and until you are ready
to actually report.

01:24:48.900 --> 01:24:56.420
So it's a way of identifying,
allowing you to kind of see

01:24:56.420 --> 01:24:58.100
what else is happening.

01:24:58.100 --> 01:25:03.410
But that's a way, that's
one way, of allowing women

01:25:03.410 --> 01:25:07.220
to record data without
going that next step,

01:25:07.220 --> 01:25:09.110
again, until you're ready.

01:25:09.110 --> 01:25:11.300
And you know, it's not perfect.

01:25:11.300 --> 01:25:15.200
But it's actually been
adopted by a number of places

01:25:15.200 --> 01:25:18.396
and is having
significant success.

01:25:18.396 --> 01:25:19.520
So that's just one example.

01:25:22.519 --> 01:25:24.060
Hi, my name is
[? Sara Silverland. ?]

01:25:24.060 --> 01:25:25.890
And I'm a grad student.

01:25:25.890 --> 01:25:28.410
And my question, you've
talked a lot about what you

01:25:28.410 --> 01:25:30.430
can do to change the culture.

01:25:30.430 --> 01:25:32.640
But what happens when
someone does come forward,

01:25:32.640 --> 01:25:35.730
and how can the community
best support them

01:25:35.730 --> 01:25:36.840
when that does happen?

01:25:36.840 --> 01:25:38.423
And that's sort of
relevant to me this

01:25:38.423 --> 01:25:40.380
me because I went to
the same high school

01:25:40.380 --> 01:25:42.570
as Christine Blasey Ford.

01:25:42.570 --> 01:25:46.530
And our alumni network has
been reeling and signing

01:25:46.530 --> 01:25:47.990
letters of support around this.

01:25:47.990 --> 01:25:51.560
And so how should we
best deal with that?

01:25:51.560 --> 01:25:54.840
Well, the first thing
I would say is, one,

01:25:54.840 --> 01:25:57.170
and I think I began with this--

01:25:57.170 --> 01:26:00.020
reporting is an act of courage.

01:26:00.020 --> 01:26:05.972
And we should support
those who report.

01:26:05.972 --> 01:26:06.930
That's the first thing.

01:26:06.930 --> 01:26:11.060
Second, if there are
incidents of sexual harassment

01:26:11.060 --> 01:26:13.310
in which the target
does not want

01:26:13.310 --> 01:26:18.320
to be public with reporting
but goes to a person

01:26:18.320 --> 01:26:21.580
to share the information, is
not ready to come forward,

01:26:21.580 --> 01:26:25.700
then we really have to be pretty
active in providing support,

01:26:25.700 --> 01:26:30.140
whether or not that person
wants to be public in reporting.

01:26:30.140 --> 01:26:32.480
And there are a
series of supports

01:26:32.480 --> 01:26:36.020
that have to do with
mental health, health care,

01:26:36.020 --> 01:26:37.784
social services, et cetera.

01:26:37.784 --> 01:26:39.200
So I think that
there are a number

01:26:39.200 --> 01:26:44.780
of ways that we need to both
honor and support targets

01:26:44.780 --> 01:26:46.375
of harassment.

01:26:49.345 --> 01:26:52.440
Hi, I'm Nick Rothbacher,
and I'm a grad student here.

01:26:52.440 --> 01:26:55.330
A lot of the recommendations
that you make in the room

01:26:55.330 --> 01:27:00.550
or require a
significant commitment

01:27:00.550 --> 01:27:03.430
from the institutions
to challenge themselves

01:27:03.430 --> 01:27:05.740
and do a lot of
things that would seem

01:27:05.740 --> 01:27:09.730
to be against the current
interests of the institution,

01:27:09.730 --> 01:27:15.160
like damaging their image,
or sanctioning members

01:27:15.160 --> 01:27:17.890
of the research community.

01:27:17.890 --> 01:27:22.360
How do you propose that we
enforce these recommendations

01:27:22.360 --> 01:27:26.540
against institutions that
often have more power than--

01:27:26.540 --> 01:27:29.060
than I can describe
in this time?

01:27:31.810 --> 01:27:34.160
Yeah, I mean, I could
sort of take that on.

01:27:34.160 --> 01:27:38.200
I mean, when we got to
the point in the report

01:27:38.200 --> 01:27:42.280
where we sort of understood
what the findings were,

01:27:42.280 --> 01:27:46.900
we sort of sat back and said,
OK, what's the solution.

01:27:46.900 --> 01:27:51.250
And the first word that
comes to mind is leadership.

01:27:51.250 --> 01:27:53.554
And you have to start there.

01:27:53.554 --> 01:27:54.970
You have to start
with leadership,

01:27:54.970 --> 01:27:58.660
with a commitment on the part
of leadership to the values

01:27:58.660 --> 01:28:00.590
that we have laid out.

01:28:00.590 --> 01:28:03.710
And so you're talking
presidents, provosts--

01:28:03.710 --> 01:28:07.600
[LAUGHS]-- I'm just kidding.

01:28:07.600 --> 01:28:10.250
I'm just kidding, Marty--

01:28:10.250 --> 01:28:12.940
deans, department heads.

01:28:12.940 --> 01:28:16.720
People who are leaders in the
community that share common

01:28:16.720 --> 01:28:23.050
values, and they can make
those values more public

01:28:23.050 --> 01:28:25.720
and then the rest
of the community

01:28:25.720 --> 01:28:29.890
will gain confidence
that this is

01:28:29.890 --> 01:28:35.080
an institution that values
the values that we've

01:28:35.080 --> 01:28:36.500
been talking about.

01:28:36.500 --> 01:28:38.830
And so that's really
where we started.

01:28:38.830 --> 01:28:40.600
And then we got quite specific.

01:28:40.600 --> 01:28:42.790
OK, what should fellow
federal agencies do?

01:28:42.790 --> 01:28:46.150
Well, what about the law, and
all these other things, but it

01:28:46.150 --> 01:28:49.760
all began with the whole
question of leadership.

01:28:49.760 --> 01:28:52.510
And I think that's probably
where the recommendations

01:28:52.510 --> 01:28:57.700
begin, is the responsibility
of academic institutions

01:28:57.700 --> 01:29:03.160
to have a safe, welcoming,
civil environment to support

01:29:03.160 --> 01:29:04.180
students.

01:29:04.180 --> 01:29:08.050
And everything else
below that are specifics

01:29:08.050 --> 01:29:09.830
that one needs to work out.

01:29:09.830 --> 01:29:11.470
But I mean, it
really starts there.

01:29:11.470 --> 01:29:14.050
So I just want to add one thing
because the question becomes,

01:29:14.050 --> 01:29:18.230
so who holds the leader,
the top leader accountable?

01:29:18.230 --> 01:29:20.050
Well, you know,
we do have bosses?

01:29:20.050 --> 01:29:22.710
And they're called
boards of trustees.

01:29:22.710 --> 01:29:25.480
At MIT, you have an
executive committee.

01:29:25.480 --> 01:29:30.010
And, you know, those
people also have

01:29:30.010 --> 01:29:33.010
to be held responsible
because then they hold

01:29:33.010 --> 01:29:35.060
the leadership responsible.

01:29:35.060 --> 01:29:38.350
These are issues that need
to be discussed and reported

01:29:38.350 --> 01:29:45.580
on openly, transparently, and
so it isn't just the leader.

01:29:45.580 --> 01:29:48.220
The leader reports
to somebody, and we

01:29:48.220 --> 01:29:51.730
need to also be talking to that
level of leadership-- which

01:29:51.730 --> 01:29:53.820
really has kind of
the ultimate power.

01:29:53.820 --> 01:29:55.600
Yeah, and I would agree.

01:29:55.600 --> 01:29:59.790
And in an ideal world,
that would work.

01:29:59.790 --> 01:30:02.730
[LAUGHTER]

01:30:02.730 --> 01:30:10.900
But very often we know
that the bosses of the boss

01:30:10.900 --> 01:30:13.180
are more conservative
about these issues,

01:30:13.180 --> 01:30:18.550
and more protective of the
institutional reputation,

01:30:18.550 --> 01:30:22.360
than even is the president.

01:30:22.360 --> 01:30:26.710
And so you have to look
at external levers.

01:30:26.710 --> 01:30:29.930
In the last few years, it's
been the Department of Education

01:30:29.930 --> 01:30:31.070
that has pushed the levers.

01:30:31.070 --> 01:30:32.380
[LAUGHTER]

01:30:32.380 --> 01:30:35.780
Not so much anymore.

01:30:35.780 --> 01:30:37.760
But there are alums.

01:30:37.760 --> 01:30:42.740
I mean, alums now
have significant power

01:30:42.740 --> 01:30:44.300
to influence--

01:30:44.300 --> 01:30:46.880
the changes, yes,
you know, right?

01:30:49.760 --> 01:30:54.760
To initiate and
instigate changes.

01:30:54.760 --> 01:30:59.510
And maybe not change
the minds or the values,

01:30:59.510 --> 01:31:04.040
but to reinforce the work
that the leaders are trying

01:31:04.040 --> 01:31:06.410
to take on and may
be getting pushback

01:31:06.410 --> 01:31:07.920
from different sources.

01:31:07.920 --> 01:31:11.540
So I think that there
are a number of ways.

01:31:11.540 --> 01:31:16.460
But on another level,
you have to convince

01:31:16.460 --> 01:31:23.330
institutional leaders that
it is in their best interests

01:31:23.330 --> 01:31:27.470
to address these problems
in this moment, in the world

01:31:27.470 --> 01:31:31.550
today, because
institutional reputation can

01:31:31.550 --> 01:31:41.330
be lost by one lawsuit that
reveals what is happening.

01:31:41.330 --> 01:31:48.160
And if a lawsuit happens, all
kinds of discovery can happen.

01:31:48.160 --> 01:31:52.030
And so it is no
longer tenable to say

01:31:52.030 --> 01:31:56.290
that you're protecting yourself
by not being transparent.

01:31:56.290 --> 01:31:59.380
And at the very
heart of this report,

01:31:59.380 --> 01:32:03.470
really, is a call
for transparency.

01:32:03.470 --> 01:32:08.280
So I think a lot of things need
to come together to be sure.

01:32:08.280 --> 01:32:12.890
And you know, the other thing
that you have is students.

01:32:12.890 --> 01:32:14.880
Students can protest.

01:32:14.880 --> 01:32:17.740
Students can make noise.

01:32:17.740 --> 01:32:20.190
And believe me, they
make more of a difference

01:32:20.190 --> 01:32:23.599
than I think they
sometimes think they have.

01:32:23.599 --> 01:32:26.140
And one other, I just wanted to
add one more lever because, I

01:32:26.140 --> 01:32:27.640
think, Anita, that
was, I mean, it's

01:32:27.640 --> 01:32:30.640
a very important
statement, which

01:32:30.640 --> 01:32:35.560
is that is exactly why we made
the recommendations on funders.

01:32:35.560 --> 01:32:41.290
If you really want a lever under
funders, your research funding,

01:32:41.290 --> 01:32:42.270
is a critical one.

01:32:42.270 --> 01:32:44.750
So that's another aspect of it.

01:32:44.750 --> 01:32:47.950
So we have two more
people at the mics.

01:32:47.950 --> 01:32:50.440
And we're going to take
these two questions,

01:32:50.440 --> 01:32:55.630
and then we're going
to be able to wrap up,

01:32:55.630 --> 01:32:57.060
Thanks again for the talk.

01:32:57.060 --> 01:33:01.170
One thing that struck me in
the presentation was that--

01:33:01.170 --> 01:33:04.020
I think someone briefly
mentioned that institutions

01:33:04.020 --> 01:33:08.850
have tried to implement policies
that reduce sexual harassment,

01:33:08.850 --> 01:33:11.610
but there is no statistically
significant decrease

01:33:11.610 --> 01:33:12.910
in sexual harassment.

01:33:12.910 --> 01:33:15.530
So how can we be sure that
institutions will ever

01:33:15.530 --> 01:33:17.280
get policies that are
actually meaningful,

01:33:17.280 --> 01:33:20.040
and not just checking the boxes
like they did in the past?

01:33:22.720 --> 01:33:24.180
Well that's the whole--

01:33:24.180 --> 01:33:28.420
I mean you're at the heart of
what we're attempting to do,

01:33:28.420 --> 01:33:31.380
which is that a lot of
the approach in the past

01:33:31.380 --> 01:33:35.730
has been one that has been
purely legalistic, avoiding

01:33:35.730 --> 01:33:37.860
risk, checking the boxes.

01:33:37.860 --> 01:33:40.500
And that really
does not do anything

01:33:40.500 --> 01:33:43.690
for changing the culture
and changing behavior.

01:33:43.690 --> 01:33:45.490
And we have the data
to support that.

01:33:45.490 --> 01:33:48.030
So I think this
really is about kind

01:33:48.030 --> 01:33:52.140
of moving away
from that mentality

01:33:52.140 --> 01:33:54.000
to a more
comprehensive approach.

01:33:58.410 --> 01:34:00.030
Hello, my name is Ashley Blum.

01:34:00.030 --> 01:34:02.075
I'm a grad student over
in the business school.

01:34:02.075 --> 01:34:03.960
I wanted to thank you
all for taking the time

01:34:03.960 --> 01:34:05.370
to share your thoughts today.

01:34:05.370 --> 01:34:06.786
I also want to
take an opportunity

01:34:06.786 --> 01:34:11.100
to thank everyone here for all
of their questions and insight.

01:34:11.100 --> 01:34:13.974
If I'm understanding kind of
the trends of this, what I feel

01:34:13.974 --> 01:34:16.140
is that we're all struggling
with kind of the rubber

01:34:16.140 --> 01:34:16.848
meeting the road.

01:34:16.848 --> 01:34:18.870
We have all these
great ideas, but it's

01:34:18.870 --> 01:34:21.337
difficult to get
them implemented.

01:34:21.337 --> 01:34:23.670
And so one of the things that
I actually wanted to offer

01:34:23.670 --> 01:34:26.310
was, in the spirit of
students making noise,

01:34:26.310 --> 01:34:28.180
a bit of a solution here.

01:34:28.180 --> 01:34:29.847
And so, at the
business school, I

01:34:29.847 --> 01:34:31.680
am part of a group
called Breaking the Mold.

01:34:31.680 --> 01:34:34.950
And in February, we're
hosting a hack for inclusion.

01:34:34.950 --> 01:34:36.450
And so this is
geared towards trying

01:34:36.450 --> 01:34:40.260
to combat bias in our schools,
communities, and in our jobs.

01:34:40.260 --> 01:34:42.690
So we are looking for
broader involvement

01:34:42.690 --> 01:34:44.420
within the academic
community, and also

01:34:44.420 --> 01:34:46.680
the Boston surrounding areas.

01:34:46.680 --> 01:34:48.720
So if you're interested,
please take a look

01:34:48.720 --> 01:34:51.780
at our web site which,
is Breaking the Mold MIT.

01:34:51.780 --> 01:34:52.447
And let us know.

01:34:52.447 --> 01:34:54.530
So thank you again for
your time, I appreciate it.

01:34:54.530 --> 01:34:55.820
That's a great way to end.

01:34:55.820 --> 01:34:57.066
Good way to end.

01:34:57.066 --> 01:35:05.550
[APPLAUSE]

01:35:05.550 --> 01:35:10.215
So I just wanted to wrap up
by saying thank you to Sheila,

01:35:10.215 --> 01:35:15.180
to Anita, and Paula,
for leading this really

01:35:15.180 --> 01:35:17.220
thoughtful, insightful,
and I would even

01:35:17.220 --> 01:35:20.190
say, hopeful discussion.

01:35:20.190 --> 01:35:23.100
You are demonstrating
the kind of leadership

01:35:23.100 --> 01:35:25.170
that your report calls for.

01:35:25.170 --> 01:35:29.880
That is so essential to
eradicating sexual harassment

01:35:29.880 --> 01:35:31.540
in academia.

01:35:31.540 --> 01:35:34.290
So through your
hard work, you are

01:35:34.290 --> 01:35:38.370
offering all of higher
ed, very importantly,

01:35:38.370 --> 01:35:41.880
an actionable roadmap to follow.

01:35:41.880 --> 01:35:45.210
And also a call to
action to answer.

01:35:45.210 --> 01:35:47.960
We're really grateful
for your example,

01:35:47.960 --> 01:35:49.520
and for your dedication.

01:35:49.520 --> 01:35:51.670
And to the audience--

01:35:51.670 --> 01:35:54.600
thank you so much for
caring about these issues,

01:35:54.600 --> 01:35:58.050
for engaging with us, and for
being committed to finding

01:35:58.050 --> 01:36:01.710
the solutions we urgently need.

01:36:01.710 --> 01:36:04.150
At the end of the
day, every one of us--

01:36:04.150 --> 01:36:05.910
and we heard this
today-- has a role

01:36:05.910 --> 01:36:09.300
to play in contributing
to the cultural changes

01:36:09.300 --> 01:36:12.360
that this report calls for.

01:36:12.360 --> 01:36:16.500
By being here today you're
sending a clear message

01:36:16.500 --> 01:36:19.750
that, together, we can
make things better.

01:36:19.750 --> 01:36:24.090
So thank you for sharing this
responsibility with all of us

01:36:24.090 --> 01:36:28.090
here at MIT and across
higher education.

01:36:28.090 --> 01:36:33.440
And thank you, again, for taking
part in this vital discussion.

